$[Import* render_lab]
$[Import* o3de_lighting]
$[Import* o3de_lighting_story]
$[Import* o3de_lighting_natives]
# How O3DE Lights a Frame {#video subtitle="The lighting model of O3DE's Atom renderer, pass by pass: ported to WGSL and read from O3DE 26.05's source" engineVersion="26.05 (2.6.0)" commit=3db6943249d8bd7960b9ed7e9aee310b7668586e date=2026-10-30 fps=30 width=1920 height=1080}

## Where the lighting runs {#c1 number=1 target=810 summary="The opaque frame of MainRenderPipeline: a depth pre-pass, tiled light culling, shadows, a forward pass into five targets, and the full-screen passes that finish its light." sources="engine-version, msaa-default, opaque-pass-order, forward-pixel, evaluate-lighting, forward-targets, target-formats, normal-encoding, forward-ibl-stencil, lighting-model-select, lowend-forward, deferred-inert"}

### Light, assembled pass by pass {#c1-open kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoRoom panelHidden 1.0 {'
    {labels 0.0}
    {forwardStage 0.0}
    {sunLux 20.0}
    {iblExposure 2.0}
    {skyboxExposure 2.0}
    {exposure 0.18}
    {sunYaw 40.0}
    {sunPitch 52.0}
    {lightOn 1.0}}}}
${cue "key" {'
    {motion {' {cameraPath {' {pose 24.0 20.0 6.2} {pose 4.0 14.0 6.6} {pose -14.0 18.0 6.0}}}}}}}

This is the lab's study room, lit by the shaders of the Open 3D Engine's Atom renderer, ported to WGSL.

${overText
    {' {sunLuxRange 20.0 40.0}}
    " A sun shines in through the open ceiling, and a small point light hangs over the floor."}

Right now we see only the light that the forward pass computed itself.

${overText
    {' {forwardStageRange 0.0 1.0}}
    " Now the sky's diffuse light is added, and the shadowed sides fill in."}

${overText
    {' {forwardStageRange 1.0 2.0}}
    " Next come the reflections of the sky, on the sphere and along the floor."}

${overText
    {' {forwardStageRange 2.0 3.0}}
    " Last, ambient occlusion darkens the creases, here where the teapot meets the floor."}

$settle Each of these parts came from a different pass, at a different time in the frame.

So this video answers one question.

How does O3DE light a room from many lights and the sky, without shading every pixel against every light?

### The default pipeline {#c1-pipeline kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoRoom panelHidden 1.0 {append baseLight {' {labels 0.0}}}}}
${cue "key" {'
    {motion {' {cameraPath {' {pose -10.0 16.0 6.2} {pose 12.0 22.0 5.6} {pose 26.0 18.0 6.4}}}}}}}

${cue "key" {' {callout "engine-version"}}}
Everything here comes from reading the source of O3DE twenty-six oh five, engine version two point six.

${cue "key" {' {callout "render-pipeline-path"}}}
On Windows, Linux, and macOS, the engine loads one render pipeline by default, which is the set of passes that draw each frame.

That pipeline is called ${code "MainRenderPipeline"}, and Android and iOS load a mobile one instead.

${cue "key" {' {callout "msaa-default"}}} It renders with multisampling, at two samples per pixel.

The lab renders four, so its edges are a little smoother.

### First, depth {#c1-map kind=2d graph=frame focus=DEPTH}

${cue "key" {' {callout "depth-prepass"}}}
First a depth pre-pass draws only the depth of every opaque mesh.

### Then the lights are sorted into tiles {#c1-map-cull kind=2d graph=frame focus="DEPTH, CULL"}

${cue "key" {' {callout "light-culling-pass"}}}
Light culling reads that depth and sorts the lights into lists, one for each tile of ${spoken
    {tilePixels}} by ${spoken {tilePixels}} pixels.

Later, each pixel reads only its own tile's list, so it meets only the lights near it.

### The sun's shadow, per pixel {#c1-map-shadow kind=2d graph=frame focus="DEPTH, SHADOW"}

${cue "key" {' {callout "fullscreen-shadow"}}}
The shadow passes draw the sun's shadow maps and turn them into a shadow value per pixel.

### The forward pass writes five targets {#c1-map-forward kind=2d graph=frame focus="CULL, SHADOW, FWD, TARGETS, LUT"}

${cue "key" {' {callout "forward-pixel"}}}
Then the forward pass shades each pixel's surface with the lights of its tile, and writes five targets.

${cue "key" {' {callout "brdf-execute-once"}}}
The forward pass also reads a BRDF look-up texture, which is built once, at start-up.

### Full-screen passes finish the light {#c1-map-after kind=2d graph=frame focus="TARGETS, DIFF, REFL, SSS, AO, MERGE, CUBES"}

${cue "key" {' {callout "opaque-pass-order"}}} Then full-screen passes read those targets.

They add the sky's diffuse light and its reflections.

Others apply subsurface scattering and ambient occlusion, which later chapters explain.

A merge pass then adds the diffuse light to the specular, and transparent meshes are drawn on top.

${cue "key" {' {callout "specular-preset"}}}
These passes read two cubemaps of the sky, which were built with the assets, long before the frame.

### Depth first, so lighting knows what is visible {#c1-prepass kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoRoom panelHidden 1.0 {append baseLight {' {labels 0.0}}}}}
${cue "key" {'
    {motion {' {cameraPath {' {pose 18.0 20.0 6.0} {pose 2.0 15.0 6.6} {pose 16.0 21.0 5.9}}}}}}}

${cue "key" {chainTarget "depth" 5.0 imageCornerTopLeft {'
    {imageFar 4.0}
    {imageTitle "views.depth_prepass"}}}}
${cue "key" {' {callout "depth-prepass"}}}
The pre-pass stores only depth, as a thirty-two bit float, with an eight bit stencil beside it that we come back to.

That depth is reversed, so a near surface stores a larger value than a far one.

${cue "key" {' {inset "depth"} {imageFar 12.0} {blend 2.0}}}
To read the depth, this view turns each stored value back into a distance from the camera.

$settle The view draws that distance as brightness, from bright at the camera to black at twelve metres.

$settle ${cue "key" {'
    {inset "depth"}
    {mark 0.05 0.66 0.95 1.0}
    {imageMarkText "views.mark_near"}
    {blend 0.6}}} So the floor in front of us, a metre or two away, is bright.

$settle ${cue "key" {'
    {inset "depth"}
    {mark 0.22 0.1 0.78 0.46}
    {imageMarkText "views.mark_far"}
    {blend 0.6}}} The back wall, several metres farther, is a darker blue.

$settle Where two surfaces at different distances meet, the brightness jumps.

$settle That jump is why the outlines of the sphere and the boxes stand out against the wall behind them.

$settle ${cue "key" {'
    {inset "depth"}
    {mark 0.0 0.0 1.0 0.1}
    {imageMarkText "views.mark_nothing_drawn"}
    {blend 0.6}}} Where no mesh was drawn, as in the sky, the depth keeps its clear value of zero.

$settle Zero is the farthest depth, because depth is reversed, so the view shows it as black.

$settle Every later pass that needs a pixel's position starts from this one depth value.

${cue "key" {' {inset "depth"} unmark}}
${cue "key" {' {inset "depth"} {imageFar 12.0} {blend 3.0}}}
${cue "key" {' {callout "forward-depth-state"}}}
The forward pass then tests each fragment against that depth, so it shades a pixel only for the surface the camera really sees.

${cue "key" {' {callout "depth-linear"}}}
Another pass turns this depth into a plain distance from the camera, for the shadow pass and ambient occlusion to read.

### Five targets, not one colour {#c1-targets kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoRoom panelValues 1.0 {append baseLight {'
    {labels 0.0}
    {readoutMode 1.0}
    {paramFormula -1.0}}}}}
${cue "key" {append teapotPoint {' {inspectObject 8.0} {pickShown 1.0}}}}
${cue "key" {'
    {motion {' {cameraPath {' {pose 10.0 14.0 5.4} {pose -6.0 10.0 5.8} {pose 8.0 16.0 5.2}}}}}}}

${cue "key" {chainTarget "t0" 0.0 imageCornerTopLeft {' {imageTitle "views.target_diffuse"}}}}
${cue "key" {' {callout "forward-targets"}}}
The forward pass writes five targets at once.

We follow one point through them, a point low on the teapot that we call P.

Each view shows one target as the pass leaves it.

$settle ${cue "key" {'
    {inset "t0"}
    {mark 0.41 0.37 0.57 0.57}
    {imageMarkText "views.mark_p"}
    {blend 0.6}}} In every view, this ring marks P's pixel.

$settle Reading the same pixel across the five views is how we follow what the forward pass stored for P.

${cue "key" {' {inset "t0"} unmark}}
${cue "key" {chainTarget "t1" 1.0 imageCornerTopLeft {'
    {imageStops 2.0}
    {imageTitle "views.target_specular"}}}}
${cue "key" {' {callout "target-formats"}}}
Diffuse light and specular light go to two separate half-float targets.

The diffuse target holds the diffuse light that the forward pass computed for each pixel.

That light is stored in half floats, so its values can go above one.

$settle The view tone maps that light, squeezing values above one into the screen's range, as the final frame would.

$settle ${cue "key" {'
    {inset "t0"}
    {mark 0.5 0.62 0.98 0.98}
    {imageMarkText "views.mark_lit"}
    {blend 0.6}}} So the floor is bright where the sun and the point light reach it.

$settle ${cue "key" {'
    {inset "t0"}
    {mark 0.3 0.5 0.62 0.72}
    {imageMarkText "views.mark_shadow"}
    {blend 0.6}}} In the sun's shadow it is black, not dark grey, because the sky's light has not been added yet.

$settle ${cue "key" {'
    {inset "t1"}
    {mark 0.68 0.05 0.97 0.62}
    {imageMarkText "views.mark_sheen"}
    {blend 0.6}}} The specular target is shown two stops brighter, because the light reflected toward us is much weaker.

$settle It holds only that reflected light, such as the highlights, and the faint shine on the walls that face the sun.

$settle That shine grows where we look along a wall at a grazing angle.

$settle Later passes add these two together with the sky's light, and apply the occlusion, to make the final pixel.

${cue "key" {' {inset "t0"} unmark}}
${cue "key" {' {inset "t1"} unmark}}
${cue "key" {chainTarget "t2" 2.0 imageCornerBottomLeft {' {imageTitle "views.target_albedo"}}}}
${cue "key" {' {callout "forward-albedo-target"}}}
The third target holds the albedo, the surface's own colour.

Its fourth channel holds the specular occlusion, how much of the reflections a crevice blocks.

$settle ${cue "key" {'
    {inset "t2"}
    {mark 0.0 0.05 0.24 0.8}
    {imageMarkText "views.mark_base_colour"}
    {blend 0.6}}} Read it as plain colours, with eight bits a channel, from zero to one.

$settle These colours are a little darker than the base colours, because the diffuse response and the material's diffuse occlusion are already multiplied in.

$settle The diffuse response is the share of light that is not reflected at the surface.

$settle So the pass that adds the sky's diffuse light has only to multiply this colour by the sky's light.

$settle That pass needs no material data of its own.

${cue "key" {' {inset "t2"} unmark}}
${cue "key" {chainTarget "t3" 3.0 imageCornerBottomLeft {'
    {imageTitle "views.target_specular_f0"}}}}
${cue "key" {' {callout "forward-f0-target"}}}
The next target holds F zero, the colour a surface reflects when it is seen head on.

Its fourth channel holds the roughness.

$settle ${cue "key" {'
    {inset "t3"}
    {mark 0.03 0.05 0.97 0.97}
    {imageMarkText "views.mark_f0_grey"}
    {blend 0.6}}} In this view, every surface is nearly black.

$settle Each is a four percent grey, which is how much light a dielectric reflects head on.

$settle That four percent is the default specular factor of one half times the maximum of eight percent.

$settle A metal would show its own colour here, because a metal's reflection is tinted by its base colour.

$settle The roughness in the fourth channel does not show in this view.

$settle The values panel lists its value for P instead.

${cue "key" {' {inset "t3"} unmark}}
${cue "key" {chainTarget "t4" 4.0 imageCornerBottomLeft {'
    {imageChannel 5.0}
    {imageTitle "views.target_normal"}}}}
${cue "key" {' {callout "normal-encoding"}}}
The last target holds the normal, packed into ten bits per channel.

The view of this target decodes the stored normal back into a direction.

$settle It draws x as red, y as green, and z as blue.

$settle A component of minus one is drawn dark, zero at half brightness, and one at full brightness.

$settle ${cue "key" {'
    {inset "t4"}
    {mark 0.05 0.62 0.95 1.0}
    {imageMarkText "views.mark_up_green"}
    {blend 0.6}}} The floor faces straight up, along y, so its green is full.

$settle The floor's red and blue sit at half, which makes a light green.

$settle ${cue "key" {'
    {inset "t4"}
    {mark 0.2 0.05 0.8 0.45}
    {imageMarkText "views.mark_toward_blue"}
    {blend 0.6}}} The back wall faces us, along z, so it is a lavender blue.

$settle ${cue "key" {'
    {inset "t4"}
    {mark 0.0 0.05 0.2 0.8}
    {imageMarkText "views.mark_x_red"}
    {blend 0.6}}} The left wall faces along x, so its red is full, and it shows pink.

$settle Curved surfaces, like the sphere, run through every colour, because their normal turns from pixel to pixel.

${cue "key" {' {inset "t4"} unmark}}
${cue "key" {' {inset "t3"} {imageChannel 4.0} {blend 1.5}}}
${cue "key" {' {callout "forward-f0-target"}}}
In the F zero target from before, the fourth channel stores roughness in only eight bits.

So the later passes see roughness to one part in two hundred and fifty-five, while the forward pass used it at full precision.

${cue "key" {' {delay 0.4} {imageHide {' "t0" "t1"}}}} Together these targets form a thin G-buffer.

It holds what the passes after the forward pass need to finish the light.

### A normal in two numbers and a sign {#c1-normal kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoRoom panelValues 0.5 {append baseLight {'
    {labels 0.0}
    {readoutMode 2.0}
    {paramFormula -1.0}}}}}
${cue "key" {append teapotPoint {' {inspectObject 8.0} {pickShown 1.0}}}}
${cue "key" {'
    {motion {' {cameraPath {' {pose 8.0 12.0 4.6} {pose -4.0 10.0 4.9} {pose 6.0 14.0 4.5}}}}}}}

${cue "key" {chainTarget "n" 4.0 imageCornerTopLeft {'
    {imageChannel 0.0}
    {imageTitle "views.target_normal"}}}}
${cue "key" {' {callout "normal-encoding"}}}
The normal target stores the normal in signed octahedral form.

This view shows the target's channels as stored.

$settle To store it, the normal is first divided by the sum of the absolute values of its three components.

$settle That puts the normal on an octahedron, the shape on which those absolute values add up to one.

${cue "key" {' {inset "n"} {imageChannel 1.0} {blend 1.2}}}
The normal's x and y are then turned by forty-five degrees and moved into the range from zero to one.

$settle Those two numbers are the red and green channels.

${cue "key" {' {inset "n"} {imageChannel 3.0} {blend 1.2}}}
The blue channel keeps only the sign of z, so a whole ten bit channel holds a single bit.

$settle That is why the colours of this view do not show a direction.

$settle A flat surface shows one flat colour, and the panel decodes P's normal.

${cue "key" {' {inset "n"} {imageChannel 4.0} {blend 1.2}}}
${cue "key" {' {callout "normal-flags"}}}
The two bit alpha channel holds two flags, which the reflection passes read.

One flag says whether those passes apply image-based light, the light of the sky, to this pixel.

The other says whether they compensate for multiple scattering, which chapter four explains.

### The forward pass leaves the sky for later {#c1-split kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoRoom panelHidden 1.0 {append baseLight {'
    {labels 0.0}
    {forwardStage 0.0}}}}}
${cue "key" {'
    {motion {' {cameraPath {' {pose 22.0 18.0 6.0} {pose -8.0 14.0 6.4} {pose 14.0 20.0 5.8}}}}}}}

${cue "key" {chainTarget "t0" 0.0 imageCornerTopLeft {' {imageTitle "views.target_diffuse"}}}}
${cue "key" {' {callout "forward-opaque"}}}
For an opaque surface, the forward pass adds only direct light, from the sun and the point light.

It also adds the light the material emits.

Here is the diffuse target again.

$settle Its shadowed sides are still black, because the sky's light is not in it yet.

${overCallout
    {' {forwardStageRange 0.0 1.0}}
    "diffuse-global"
    "The diffuse light of the sky is added afterwards, by a full-screen pass that reads the albedo and normal targets."}

${overCallout
    {' {forwardStageRange 1.0 2.0}}
    "reflection-global"
    "The sky's reflections come from another chain of full-screen passes, which read F zero, roughness, and the normal."}

${cue "key" {' {callout "ibl-forward-transparent"}}}
Only transparent materials and skin materials take image-based light inside the forward pass itself.

A material with a clear coat, a second glossy layer on top, brings in just the specular part of the sky's light there.

### Stencil bits route each pixel {#c1-stencil kind=3d}

${cue "key" {append baseLight {'
    demoRoom
    {callout ""}
    {cameraDrift 1.0}
    {labels 0.0}
    panelHidden
    {forwardStage 3.0}
    {imageHide {' "t0"}}
    {sphereClearCoat 0.0}}}}
${cue "key" {'
    {motion {' {cameraPath {' {pose 16.0 18.0 6.0} {pose 0.0 12.0 6.4} {pose 18.0 20.0 5.8}}}}}}}

${cue "key" {chainTarget "st" 6.0 imageCornerTopLeft {' {imageTitle "views.stencil"}}}}
${cue "key" {' {callout "stencil-refs"}}}
Each mesh also writes a stencil value, a byte per pixel, and the full-screen passes test it to choose which pixels they shade.

${cue "key" {' {callout "forward-ibl-stencil"}}}
The stencil's top bit is worth one hundred and twenty-eight, and when it is set, the pass for the sky's diffuse light shades the pixel.

Its two lowest bits are worth three together, and when they are set, the reflection passes shade the pixel.

$settle ${cue "key" {'
    {inset "st"}
    {mark 0.0 0.0 1.0 0.1}
    {imageMarkText "views.mark_nothing_drawn"}
    {blend 0.6}}} In the stencil view, black is a pixel that nothing drew, so its stencil stays zero.

$settle Every mesh writes its own reference value, and the view gives each value a colour.

$settle Violet would mean the top bit alone, so the sky's diffuse light but no reflections.

$settle ${cue "key" {'
    {inset "st"}
    {mark 0.04 0.1 0.96 1.0}
    {imageMarkText "views.mark_meshes"}
    {blend 0.6}}} Teal means the top bit together with the two low bits, so the pixel gets both passes.

$settle Every mesh here is teal.

$settle Later, the reflection probes add bits of their own, and the view draws those pixels brighter.

${cue "key" {' {inset "st"} unmark}}
${overCallout
    {' {sphereClearCoatRange 0.0 1.0}}
    "clear-coat-forward-ibl"
    "Give the sphere a clear coat, and its low bits drop to zero, because it now takes its reflections inside the forward pass."}

${cue "key" {' {sphereClearCoat 0.0} {blend 1.5}}}
So the stencil keeps a pixel's reflections from being added twice, once in the forward pass and once after it.

### A material's lighting model picks its shaders {#c1-models kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoRoom panelHidden 1.0 {append baseLight {'
    {labels 1.0}
    {sphereClearCoat 0.0}}}}}
${cue "key" {'
    {motion {' {cameraPath {' {pose 20.0 20.0 6.2} {pose -6.0 14.0 6.6} {pose 12.0 18.0 6.0}}}}}}}

${cue "key" {' {callout "lighting-model-select"}}}
Every material type names a lighting model, and that name decides which shaders the engine builds for it.

${cue "key" {' {callout "lighting-models"}}}
O3DE has four lighting models, named Base, Standard, Enhanced, and Skin.

Every surface in this room uses Standard, the model of the ${code "StandardPBR"} material type.

That is the material type of the engine's default materials.

${cue "key" {' {callout "enhanced-draw-list"}}}
Enhanced and Skin add subsurface scattering and transmission, so they draw in a second forward pass that writes one more target.

### One target on mobile; deferred, but off {#c1-alternatives kind=2d diagram=forward-one-target focus=FWD1}

${cue "key" {' {callout "lowend-forward"}}}
The low-end and mobile pipelines instead use a classic forward pass, which finishes each pixel in one go.

It writes one colour target, and it adds the sky's light inside that same pass.

${cue "key" {' {callout "mobile-pipeline"}}}
The mobile pipeline has no depth pre-pass and no light culling at all.

Without culling, it caps each kind of simple light at five.

Simple lights are the point and spot lights that have no size.

So no pixel loops over more than five lights of one kind.

${cue "key" {' {callout "deferred-inert"}}}
O3DE also contains code for deferred shading through a visibility buffer.

But no pass draws it, and the console variable that would turn it on is off, so nothing in this video uses it.

### What the forward pass leaves behind {#c1-recap kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoRoom panelHidden 1.0 {append baseLight {'
    {labels 0.0}
    {forwardStage 3.0}}}}}
${cue "key" {append teapotPoint {' {inspectObject 8.0} {pickShown 1.0}}}}
${cue "key" {'
    {motion {' {cameraPath {' {pose 22.0 20.0 6.2} {pose 2.0 14.0 6.6} {pose -12.0 18.0 6.0}}}}}}}

${cue "key" {chainTarget "t0" 0.0 imageCornerTopLeft {' {imageTitle "views.target_diffuse"}}}}
So the forward pass does not finish a pixel.

It leaves direct light in two targets, and the surface in three more.

${cue "key" {chainTarget "t4" 4.0 imageCornerTopLeft {'
    {imageChannel 5.0}
    {imageTitle "views.target_normal"}}}}
The passes after it read those targets to add the sky, the reflections, and the occlusion.

$settle Before any of that, the engine has to decide which lights reach each tile, and that is the next part.

### Where to read {#c1-sources kind=2d sources="render-pipeline-path, msaa-default, opaque-pass-order, depth-prepass, forward-pixel, evaluate-lighting, forward-targets, normal-encoding, stencil-refs, lighting-model-select, deferred-inert"}

These are the files and lines this chapter followed, in reading order.

Every claim in this video cites a file, a symbol, and a line in the O3DE checkout.

The build checks that each cited line is still there.

## Direct light: which lights reach a tile {#c2 number=2 target=635 summary="Tiled light culling: each 16 x 16 tile's depth range and mask, its depth bins, the lists the forward pass walks, and how each light type is evaluated." sources="light-culling-pass, light-culling-tile, tile-prepare, nvlc-constants, is-object-inside-tile, cull-point-light, light-culling-main, light-culling-remap, tile-iterator, point-light-radius, sphere-light-apply, polygon-not-culled"}

### Every tile gets its own light list {#c2-why kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoCulling panelHidden 1.0 {'
    {labels 0.0}
    {extraLights 0.0}
    {cullTile 0.0}
    {lightMarkers 1.0}
    viewModeFinal
    {splitView 0.0}}}}
${cue "key" {append teapotPoint {' {inspectObject 8.0} {pickShown 1.0}}}}
${cue "key" {'
    {motion {' {cameraPath {' {pose 18.0 22.0 6.4} {pose 2.0 16.0 6.8} {pose 16.0 24.0 6.2}}}}}}}

The frame's first lighting step runs before any surface is shaded.

It decides which lights each part of the screen needs.

${overText
    {' {extraLightsRange 0.0 24.0}}
    " Here are twenty-four small point lights over the floor."}

Each of them reaches only part of the room, so shading every pixel against all of them would waste most of the work.

${cue "key" {' {splitView 1.0}}}
${cue "key" {' {callout "light-culling-tile"}}}
So the screen is cut into tiles of ${spoken {tilePixels}} by ${spoken {tilePixels}} pixels, and each tile keeps only the lights that can reach it.

${cue "key" {' {callout "light-culling-tile"}}}
At nineteen twenty by ten eighty, that makes one hundred and twenty by sixty-eight tiles.

That is eight thousand one hundred and sixty tiles, each with its own lists.

The screen is now split in two, and the left view colours each pixel by how many lights the culling kept for it.

$settle Each tile's list is split again by depth, into slabs called depth bins, and a pixel counts only the bin its depth falls in.

$settle ${cue "key" {'
    {label "few"}
    {labelAt {at 1.8 2.4 -2.95}}
    {labelText "views.mark_few_lights"}
    {blend 0.6}}} In this left view, dark blue means no lights at all.

$settle High on the back wall, every light is out of reach.

$settle Teal is a few, and the ramp runs up to yellow at eight or more.

$settle ${cue "key" {'
    {label "many"}
    {labelAt {at -1.05 1.3 -0.55}}
    {labelText "views.mark_many_lights"}
    {blend 0.6}}} Over the cluster of lights around the sphere, tiles keep eight or more.

$settle So two pixels of one tile can show different counts, when they lie in different depth bins.

$settle The right view shows the lit frame from the same camera, so each tile on the left lies over the same pixels on the right.

${cue "key" {' {labelHide {' "few" "many"}}}}
${cue "key" {' {motion {' {lasting sentence {sway extraLightMotion 60.0 9.0}}}}}}
As the lights move, each tile's count changes, low where nothing is near and high over the cluster.

### P's tile: its depth range {#c2-tile kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoCulling panelValues 0.6 {'
    {labels 0.0}
    {extraLights 24.0}
    {cullTile 1.0}
    {lightMarkers 1.0}}}}
${cue "key" {append teapotPoint {' {inspectObject 8.0} {pickShown 1.0}}}}
${cue "key" {'
    {motion {' {cameraPath {' {pose 12.0 18.0 5.6} {pose -4.0 14.0 6.0} {pose 10.0 20.0 5.4}}}}}}}

${cue "key" {' {callout "tile-prepare"}}}
We follow the tile that holds P, the point low on the teapot.

First, a compute pass reads the depth of the tile's two hundred and fifty-six pixels.

${cue "key" {' {callout "tile-prepare"}}} Each sample's depth becomes a distance from the camera.

The tile keeps the nearest and the farthest of those distances, called z near and z far.

${cue "key" {chainTarget "d" 5.0 imageCornerBottomLeft {'
    {imageFar 8.0}
    {imageTitle "views.depth_prepass"}}}}
${cue "key" {' {callout "tile-prepare"}}}
The pass reads both multisampling samples of each pixel, so a thin edge still counts.

This depth view is drawn as in chapter one, but black now sits at eight metres.

### A 32-bit mask of the depths actually used {#c2-mask kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoCulling panelValues 0.5 {'
    {labels 0.0}
    {extraLights 24.0}
    {cullTile 1.0}
    {lightMarkers 1.0}}}}
${cue "key" {append teapotPoint {' {inspectObject 8.0} {pickShown 1.0}}}}
${cue "key" {'
    {motion {' {cameraPath {' {pose 30.0 16.0 5.0} {pose 44.0 12.0 5.4} {pose 30.0 18.0 4.8}}}}}}}

${cue "key" {' {callout "nvlc-constants"}}}
To see which depths are used, the tile splits the span between z near and z far into thirty-two equal slices.

${cue "key" {' {callout "tile-prepare"}}} Each pixel sets the bit of the slice its depth falls in.

The bits of the whole tile are combined into one thirty-two bit mask.

${cue "key" {' {callout "is-object-inside-tile"}}}
If a tile spans the teapot and the far wall, its mask has bits only at the teapot's depth and the wall's.

$settle The empty space between them stays clear.

$settle That clear gap is what lets the culling reject a light that floats between the two.

### One to eight bins, from the span in metres {#c2-bins kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoCulling panelValues 0.3 {'
    {labels 0.0}
    {extraLights 24.0}
    {cullTile 1.0}
    {lightMarkers 1.0}}}}
${cue "key" {append teapotPoint {' {inspectObject 8.0} {pickShown 1.0}}}}

${cue "key" {'
    {inset "bins"}
    imageFromTileGrid
    {imageMode 2.0}
    imageCornerTopLeft
    {imageTitle "views.tile_bins"}}}
${cue "key" {' {callout "tile-prepare"}}}
The tile also chooses how many depth bins its lists are split into, either one, two, four, or ${spoken
    {maxBins}}.

The new view in the corner shades each tile by its number of depth bins.

$settle One bin is dark, and two, four, and eight are brighter in turn.

$settle Most tiles cover one surface and keep a single bin.

$settle ${cue "key" {'
    {inset "bins"}
    {mark 0.25 0.32 0.85 0.62}
    {imageMarkText "views.mark_more_bins"}
    {blend 0.6}}} The bright ones run along edges, where one tile spans the sphere and the wall behind it.

$settle There, more bins mean that a pixel at the front reads only the lights near the front.

${cue "key" {' {inset "bins"} unmark}}
${cue "key" {' {cameraDistance 4.0}}}
${cue "key" {' {glide cameraDistance 9.0 sentence}}}
${cue "key" {' {callout "tile-prepare"}}}
The count comes from the tile's depth span, in metres.

To choose the count, the pass takes the base two log of the span, rounds it, clamps it, and raises two to that power.

So a span under one point four one metres gets one bin.

Each doubling of that span doubles the bins, up to ${spoken {maxBins}} beyond five point six six metres.

So no bin is deeper than about one and a half metres, for any tile up to about eleven metres deep.

${cue "key" {' {cameraDistance 6.0} {blend 2.0}}}
Tiles over a single surface keep one bin, and tiles that cross from the teapot to the far wall get more.

Each bin gets its own list, so a pixel reads only the lights near its own depth.

### A light is kept by its sphere, then by the mask {#c2-cull kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoCulling panelValues 0.6 {'
    {labels 0.0}
    {extraLights 24.0}
    {cullTile 1.0}
    {lightMarkers 1.0}}}}
${cue "key" {append teapotPoint {' {inspectObject 8.0} {pickShown 1.0}}}}
${cue "key" {'
    {motion {' {cameraPath {' {pose 16.0 20.0 6.0} {pose 0.0 14.0 6.4} {pose 14.0 22.0 5.8}}}}}}}

${cue "key" {' {callout "light-culling-main"}}}
The culling pass runs one thread group of two hundred and fifty-six threads for each tile.

Each thread takes every two hundred and fifty-sixth light, so together the threads test every light once.

${cue "key" {' {callout "cull-point-light"}}}
Each tile has a box, the region of view space it covers between z near and z far.

A point light is kept when its sphere of influence touches that box.

${cue "key" {' {callout "is-object-inside-tile"}}}
Then the light's depth range is turned into slice bits and tested against the tile's mask.

If the light's bits and the mask share no bit, the light is dropped.

${overText {' {extraLightRadiusRange 0.6 1.8}} " A larger radius reaches more tiles and more bins."}

${cue "key" {' {extraLightRadius 1.0} {blend 1.5}}}
The test also records which of the tile's bins the light overlaps, as eight bits, one per bin.

### Seven groups, each closed by a marker {#c2-list kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoCulling panelHidden 0.6 {'
    {labels 0.0}
    {extraLights 24.0}
    {cullTile 1.0}
    {lightMarkers 1.0}}}}
${cue "key" {append teapotPoint {' {inspectObject 8.0} {pickShown 1.0}}}}
${cue "key" {'
    {motion {' {cameraPath {' {pose 16.0 20.0 6.0} {pose 4.0 15.0 6.3} {pose 14.0 21.0 5.9}}}}}}}

${cue "key" {'
    {inset "list"}
    imageFromLightList
    imageCornerBottomLeft
    {imageTitle "views.light_list"}}}
${cue "key" {' {callout "light-index-pack"}}}
Each kept light becomes one entry in the tile's list.

The entry packs the light's index into its upper sixteen bits and the eight bin bits below them, so one number carries both.

$settle ${cue "key" {'
    {inset "list"}
    {mark 0.0 0.0 1.0 0.13}
    {imageMarkText "views.mark_tile_list"}
    {blend 0.6}}} A new view shows the lists, and it reads from the top.

$settle The first row is the tile's list as the culling wrote it, with one cell per light it kept.

$settle Each cell is coloured by its light's type.

$settle The lights of one type form a group, and a darker cell, a marker, closes each group.

$settle ${cue "key" {'
    {inset "list"}
    {mark 0.0 0.14 1.0 1.0}
    {imageMarkText "views.mark_bin_rows"}
    {blend 0.6}}} Below that first row are the lists for the depth bins, one row per bin.

$settle Each row holds only the lights that overlap its bin, and it ends in a black end-of-list marker.

$settle ${cue "key" {'
    {inset "list"}
    {mark 0.0 0.14 0.1 0.3}
    {imageMarkText "views.mark_p_bin"}
    {blend 0.6}}} The row marked on the left is P's bin, the one list the forward pass will read for P.

${cue "key" {' {inset "list"} unmark}}
${cue "key" {' {callout "light-culling-main"}}}
The culling writes the list in seven groups, one for each kind of entry.

Decals, textures projected onto surfaces, come first, then simple point lights, simple spot lights, and sphere, disk, capsule, and quad lights.

Each group ends in its marker, so the forward pass can run a separate loop for each type.

${cue "key" {' {callout "light-culling-remap"}}}
A second pass, the remap, then copies each entry into the list of every bin it overlaps.

That makes up to ${spoken {maxBins}} lists of up to two hundred and fifty-six entries, each ended by an end-of-list marker.

${cue "key" {' {callout "nvlc-constants"}}}
At most two hundred and fifty-five of a list's entries survive, and the seven group markers take seven of them.

So a tile can keep at most two hundred and forty-eight lights and decals.

Beyond that, new entries overwrite the last slot, and the code itself expects flickering.

### The forward pass walks its bin's list {#c2-iterate kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoCulling panelHidden 0.4 {'
    {labels 0.0}
    {extraLights 24.0}
    {cullTile 1.0}
    {lightMarkers 1.0}}}}
${cue "key" {append teapotPoint {' {inspectObject 8.0} {pickShown 1.0}}}}
${cue "key" {'
    {motion {' {cameraPath {' {pose 12.0 18.0 5.6} {pose 2.0 15.0 5.9} {pose 12.0 19.0 5.5}}}}}}}

${cue "key" {'
    {inset "list"}
    imageFromLightList
    {imageWalked 0.0}
    imageCornerBottomLeft
    {imageTitle "views.light_list"}}}
${cue "key" {' {callout "tile-iterator"}}}
In the forward pass, P finds its tile, then the bin its own depth falls in.

$settle ${cue "key" {'
    {inset "list"}
    {mark 0.0 0.14 0.45 0.3}
    {imageMarkText "views.mark_read_so_far"}
    {blend 0.6}}} In P's row, the lit cells are the entries the loop has read so far.

$settle The dim cells are still ahead of the loop.

$settle When the loop reaches the black end-of-list marker, it stops.

${cue "key" {' {inset "list"} unmark}}
${cue "key" {' {inset "list"} {imageWalked 12.0} {blend 6.0}}}
${cue "key" {' {callout "tile-iterator"}}}
The forward pass reads that bin's list one group at a time.

Each light type has its own loop, which reads indices until it meets its group's marker.

${cue "key" {' {inset "list"} {imageWalked 24.0} {blend 3.0}}}
${cue "key" {' {callout "point-light-loop"}}}
For each index, the loop loads the light and adds its diffuse and specular light at P.

${cue "key" {' {callout "directional-lux"}}} Directional lights are not culled at all.

Every pixel loops over the directional lights first, before its tile's list.

### Every light fades to zero at its radius {#c2-falloff kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoPointlight panelValues 0.6 {' {labels 0.0}}}}
${cue "key" {'
    {motion {' {cameraPath {' {pose 12.0 28.0 7.2} {pose -4.0 22.0 7.6} {pose 10.0 30.0 7.0}}}}}}}

${cue "key" {' {callout "point-light-radius"}}}
A light's intensity falls as one over the distance squared.

It is also multiplied by a window, a factor that falls smoothly to zero exactly at the light's attenuation radius.

The window starts from the distance squared over the radius squared, a ratio that reaches one at the radius.

The code squares that ratio, subtracts it from one, and squares the result.

${overCallout
    {' {attenuationRadiusRange 2.0 6.0}}
    "point-light-radius"
    "So the light ends exactly where the sphere that the culling tested ends."}

${cue "key" {' {attenuationRadius 3.5} {blend 1.5}}}
${cue "key" {' {callout "point-light-auto-radius"}}}
In its automatic mode, the editor sets that radius from the light's brightness.

The radius is the distance where the light's intensity falls to a cutoff of zero point one.

### Spheres, disks, capsules, quads: one approximation each {#c2-types kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoSpecular panelValues 0.6 {'
    {labels 0.0}
    {sphereLight 1.0}
    {sphereLightBulb 0.05}}}}
${cue "key" {'
    {motion {' {cameraPath {' {pose 8.0 18.0 3.4} {pose -6.0 14.0 3.7} {pose 6.0 20.0 3.3}}}}}}}

${cue "key" {' {callout "area-lights"}}}
Lights that have a size are evaluated in closed form, each with its own approximation.

${overCallout
    {' {sphereLightBulbRange 0.05 0.15}}
    "sphere-light-apply"
    "A sphere light's specular is computed as if all its light came from one point on the sphere."}

${overText
    {' {sphereLightBulbRange 0.15 0.4}}
    "That point is the one nearest the reflection ray, so a bigger bulb widens the highlight"}.

${overCallout
    {' {sphereRoughnessRange 0.2 0.6}}
    "sphere-light-apply"
    "The light's intensity is then normalised by the roughness, so the wider highlight does not add energy."}

${cue "key" {' {callout "area-lights"}}} Disks and capsules work the same way.

Quads use linearly transformed cosines, which warp a simple cosine lobe into the shape of the BRDF.

A cosine lobe has a closed-form integral over a quad, so the warped lobe does too.

The fitted warps are stored in two thirty-two by thirty-two tables.

### Read from the code: two lights that do not behave {#c2-findings kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoCulling panelHidden 0.6 {'
    {labels 0.0}
    {extraLights 24.0}
    {cullTile 0.0}
    {lightMarkers 1.0}
    viewModeFinal
    {splitView 0.0}}}}
${cue "key" {append teapotPoint {' {inspectObject 8.0} {pickShown 1.0}}}}
${cue "key" {'
    {motion {' {cameraPath {' {pose 18.0 22.0 6.4} {pose 2.0 16.0 6.8} {pose 16.0 24.0 6.2}}}}}}}

${cue "key" {'
    {inset "list"}
    imageFromLightList
    imageCornerBottomLeft
    {imageTitle "views.light_list"}}}
${cue "key" {' {callout "polygon-not-culled"}}}
The code shows that polygon lights, area lights shaped as polygons, have no group in the list.

The forward pass's polygon loop starts after the quad group's marker, meets the end of the list at once, and lights nothing.

So with culling on, a polygon light is never shaded.

${cue "key" {' {callout "directional-channel-return"}}}
A light can be put on a lighting channel, so that it lights only surfaces on the same channel.

In the directional loop, a light on another channel should simply be skipped.

Instead, the code exits the loop, so every directional light after it is lost too.

${cue "key" {' {callout "directional-angular-unused"}}}
The code also adjusts the sun's direction for the sun's angular size, its size in the sky.

But the specular uses the plain direction instead, so the sun's size changes nothing.

These findings come from reading O3DE's source, because the lab does not run the engine itself.

### The lights each pixel will see {#c2-recap kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoCulling panelHidden 1.0 {'
    {labels 0.0}
    {extraLights 24.0}
    {cullTile 0.0}
    {lightMarkers 1.0}
    viewModeFinal
    {splitView 0.0}}}}
${cue "key" {'
    {motion {' {cameraPath {' {pose 18.0 22.0 6.4} {pose -2.0 16.0 6.8} {pose 14.0 24.0 6.2}}}}}}}

${cue "key" {' {splitView 1.0}}}
So culling turns all the scene's lights into one short list per tile and depth bin, before a single pixel is shaded.

The left view shows each tile's count again, and the right view shows the frame those lists light.

That keeps the forward pass's cost close to the lights a pixel can really see.

$settle With the lights sorted into lists, the next part turns to what they light, the surface.

### Where to read {#c2-sources kind=2d sources="light-culling-pass, light-culling-tile, tile-prepare, nvlc-constants, is-object-inside-tile, cull-point-light, light-culling-main, light-index-pack, light-culling-remap, tile-iterator, point-light-radius, sphere-light-apply, polygon-not-culled"}

