## The surface: from material to albedo, F0, roughness {#c3 number=3 target=335 summary="How StandardPBR's inputs become a surface: the metallic workflow, roughness and its clamps, specular anti-aliasing, occlusion and emission, and the decals that change the surface after its lighting is set up." sources="surface-eval, set-albedo-f0, material-utils, roughness-values, specular-aa, occlusion-input, decal-apply"}

### The material's inputs become a surface {#c3-inputs kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoSurface panelValues 0.6 {' {labels 1.0}}}}
${cue "key" {'
    {motion {' {cameraPath {' {pose 4.0 8.0 3.1} {pose -10.0 5.0 3.3} {pose 6.0 10.0 3.0}}}}}}}

The lights are now sorted, so we turn to the surfaces they light.

${cue "key" {' panelCode listingSurfaceSetup {highlightCode "surface-setup" {' "albedo"}}}}
${cue "key" {' {callout "surface-eval"}}}
For each pixel, the material first evaluates a surface, a small set of numbers the lighting reads.

They are the base colour, metallic, the specular factor that scales F zero, roughness, the normal, and the occlusion.

${cue "key" {chainTarget "a" 2.0 imageCornerTopLeft {' {imageTitle "views.target_albedo"}}}}
${cue "key" {' {callout "base-colour-acescg"}}}
A colour property is first converted from linear sRGB into ACEScg, the colour space the lighting works in.

The albedo view shows the colour after that conversion, as the lighting uses it.

### Albedo and F0 from one base colour {#c3-metal kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoSurface panelValues 0.5 {' {labels 1.0}}}}
${cue "key" {' {motion {' {cameraPath surfaceSweep}}}}}

${cue "key" {chainTarget "a" 2.0 imageCornerTopLeft {' {imageTitle "views.target_albedo"}}}}
${cue "key" {chainTarget "f" 3.0 imageCornerTopLeft {' {imageTitle "views.target_specular_f0"}}}}
${cue "key" {' {callout "set-albedo-f0"}}}
One base colour feeds two outputs.

One is the albedo, which colours the diffuse light, and the other is F zero, the colour of the reflection head on.

${overCallout
    {' {sphereMetallicRange 0.0 1.0}}
    "material-utils"
    "As metallic rises, the albedo fades to black and F zero turns from a dielectric's grey to the base colour itself."}

$settle ${cue "key" {'
    {inset "a"}
    {mark 0.28 0.42 0.62 0.98}
    {imageMarkText "views.mark_albedo"}
    {blend 0.6}}} Watch the sphere in the albedo target, on the left.

$settle As metallic rises, the sphere goes from its base colour to black.

$settle ${cue "key" {'
    {inset "f"}
    {mark 0.28 0.42 0.62 0.98}
    {imageMarkText "views.mark_f0"}
    {blend 0.6}}} In F zero, beside it, the same sphere goes from four percent grey to its base colour.

$settle The colour has moved from the diffuse light to the reflection, and that move is what metallic means here.

${cue "key" {' {inset "a"} unmark}}
${cue "key" {' {inset "f"} unmark}}
${cue "key" {' {sphereMetallic 0.0} {blend 1.0}}}
${overCallout
    {' {specularF0FactorRange 0.0 1.0}}
    "material-utils"
    "A dielectric's F zero is zero point zero eight times the specular factor."}

${cue "key" {' {specularF0Factor 0.5} {blend 1.0}}}
So the default factor, a half, gives the four percent grey we saw.

${overText {' {sphereMetallicRange 0.0 1.0}} " A metal therefore has no diffuse light at all."}

All of a metal's colour is in its reflection.

### Roughness, clamped and squared twice {#c3-rough kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoSurface panelValues 0.5 {' {labels 1.0}}}}
${cue "key" {' {motion {' {cameraPath surfaceSweep}}}}}

${cue "key" {' panelCode listingSurfaceSetup {highlightCode "surface-setup" {' "roughness"}}}}
${cue "key" {' {callout "roughness-values"}}}
The authored roughness is clamped to at least zero point zero two four, so no highlight becomes infinitely sharp.

${overCallout
    {' {sphereRoughnessRange 0.05 0.8}}
    "roughness-values"
    "That roughness is squared into a second value, roughness A, which the microfacet formulas use as the lobe's width."}

Roughness A is squared again into a third value, called roughness A two.

${cue "key" {' {sphereRoughness 0.35} {blend 1.5}}}
Each later formula picks the form it needs from these three numbers.

### Specular AA widens roughness where the normal changes fast {#c3-aa kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoSurface panelValues 0.3 {'
    {labels 0.0}
    {sphereRoughness 0.1}
    {sphereMetallic 1.0}}}}
${cue "key" {' {cameraDistance 6.0} {cameraYaw 4.0} {cameraPitch 8.0}}}

${cue "key" {' {callout "specular-aa"}}}
Far away, a smooth sphere's highlight covers less than a pixel, and it flickers as the camera moves.

${cue "key" {' {glide cameraDistance 11.0 sentence}}}
${cue "key" {' {callout "specular-aa"}}}
Specular anti-aliasing adds roughness where the normal changes fast from one pixel to the next.

The code measures how much the normal changes between neighbouring pixels, and squares it.

A quarter of that squared change is treated as a variance, the spread of normals within one pixel.

Twice that variance is added to roughness A two.

The wider highlight then covers more pixels, so it no longer vanishes between them.

${cue "key" {' {callout "specular-aa-default"}}}
Specular anti-aliasing is on by default, and the extra roughness is capped at zero point one eight.

${overText {' {specularAARange 1.0 0.0}} " Switched off, the far highlight thins and sparkles."}

${cue "key" {' {specularAA 1.0} {cameraDistance 3.1} {blend 2.0}}}
Close up, the normal changes slowly from pixel to pixel, so much less is added.

### Occlusion maps, and emission {#c3-occl kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoSurface panelValues 0.5 {'
    {labels 1.0}
    {specularAA 1.0}
    {sphereRoughness 0.35}
    {sphereMetallic 0.0}}}}
${cue "key" {' {motion {' {cameraPath surfaceSweep}}}}}

${overCallout
    {' {sphereOcclusionRange 0.0 1.0}}
    "occlusion-input"
    "An occlusion map's red channel is raised to the power of its factor."}

So a factor of one keeps the map as it is, and a factor of zero ignores it.

${overCallout
    {' {diffuseOcclusionFactorRange 0.0 2.0}}
    "occlusion-input"
    "With a map, the factor only strengthens or weakens the map's darkening."}

Without a map, the occlusion is one, and one to any power is still one, so the factor does nothing.

${cue "key" {' {sphereOcclusion 0.0} {blend 1.0}}}
${cue "key" {' {callout "emissive-input"}}}
Emission is authored in EV100, a photographic exposure scale, and converted to nits, a unit of luminance.

It is added after the lighting, so it shines even where no light reaches.

### A decal changes the surface, after its lighting is set up {#c3-decal kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoDecals panelValues 0.5 {' {labels 0.0}}}}
${cue "key" {'
    {motion {' {cameraPath {' {pose 10.0 55.0 2.4} {pose -6.0 48.0 2.7} {pose 8.0 58.0 2.3}}}}}}}

${cue "key" {' {callout "decal-apply"}}}
A decal projects a texture through a box onto whatever surface lies inside it.

${cue "key" {' {decalOpacity 0.0}}}
${overCallout
    {' {decalOpacityRange 0.0 1.0}}
    "decal-apply"
    "Inside the box, the decal's colour replaces the albedo, as far as its opacity allows."}

The decal's normal map bends the normal too.

Roughness and F zero stay as they were, so a decal cannot change how shiny a surface is.

${overCallout
    {' {decalTiltRange 0.0 60.0}}
    "decal-attenuation"
    "A decal also fades as the surface turns away from its projection, by a smoothstep of the angle."}

${cue "key" {' {decalTilt 0.0} {blend 1.0}}}
${cue "key" {' {callout "evaluate-lighting"}}}
In O3DE twenty-six oh five, the forward pass first sets up its lighting data from the original surface.

Only then does it apply the decals, to a copy of the surface that the lights read.

${cue "key" {chainTarget "a" 2.0 imageCornerTopLeft {' {imageTitle "views.target_albedo"}}}}
${cue "key" {' {callout "forward-albedo-target"}}}
The code shows that the direct lights see the decal.

But the targets are written from the original surface, from before the decal.

So the sky's light, which a full-screen pass adds later from those targets, ignores the decal.

$settle ${cue "key" {'
    {inset "a"}
    {mark 0.08 0.6 0.92 1.0}
    {imageMarkText "views.mark_no_decal"}
    {blend 0.6}}} In the albedo view, the floor under the decals is plain, with none of the decals' paint or grid lines.

$settle So under a decal, the sky's diffuse light and the reflections still use the colour and the normal from before the decal.

### A surface, ready to be lit {#c3-recap kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoSurface panelValues 1.0 {' {labels 1.0}}}}
${cue "key" {'
    {motion {' {cameraPath {' {pose 4.0 8.0 3.1} {pose -10.0 6.0 3.3} {pose 8.0 10.0 3.0}}}}}}}

${overText
    {' {sphereRoughnessRange 0.1 0.7}}
    " So a pixel's surface comes down to an albedo, an F zero, three forms of one roughness, a normal, and two occlusion factors."}

One occlusion factor is for diffuse light, and the other is for specular light.

${overText {' {sphereMetallicRange 0.0 1.0}} " Every light will be weighed against these numbers."}

The forward pass's targets keep these numbers for the passes that come after it.

$settle The next part follows what each light does with them, through the BRDF.

### Where to read {#c3-sources kind=2d sources="surface-eval, base-colour-acescg, set-albedo-f0, material-utils, roughness-values, specular-aa, specular-aa-default, occlusion-input, emissive-input, decal-apply, evaluate-lighting"}

These are the files and lines this chapter followed, in reading order.

## The BRDF: how the surface answers each light {#c4 number=4 target=490 summary="GGX specular and its D, G, and F, the Lambertian diffuse and its response, the per-light formula, the four lighting models, the BRDF look-up texture, and multiscatter compensation, which is off unless a material turns it on." sources="specular-ggx, ggx-d, ggx-g, fresnel-schlick, diffuse-lambert, fresnel-roughness, point-light-apply, standard-brdf, brdf-texture, multiscatter, ms-material-property"}

### Four directions at a point {#c4-vectors kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoSpecular panelValues 0.5 {' {vectors 1.0} {lobe 0.0} {paramFormula 0.0}}}}
${cue "key" {'
    {motion {'
        {cameraPath {' {pose 0.0 6.0 3.1} {pose -12.0 4.0 3.3} {pose 6.0 8.0 3.0}}}
        {pingPonging {eased easingSine {orbit {' lightX lightY} {' -1.05 1.24} 70.0 beat}}}}}}}

The surface is now a set of numbers.

The BRDF turns those numbers, the light's direction, and the eye's direction into reflected light.

${cue "key" {' {vectors 0.0}}}
${overCallout
    {' {vectorsRange 0.0 1.0}}
    "specular-ggx"
    "At the top of this sphere are the normal N, the direction L to the light, and the direction V to the eye."}

H is the direction halfway between L and V.

${cue "key" {' {callout "specular-ggx"}}} As the light swings, H swings with it.

Every term of the specular reflection is measured against these four directions.

### D: how many microfacets face H {#c4-d kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoSpecular panelValues 0.4 {'
    {vectors 1.0}
    {lobe 1.0}
    {paramFormula 1.0}
    {term 1}}}}
${cue "key" {' {motion {' {cameraPath brdfSweep}}}}}

${cue "key" {' panelCode listingSpecularGgx {highlightCode "specular-ggx" {' "d"}}}}
${cue "key" {' {callout "ggx-d"}}}
The surface is modelled as tiny mirrors, called microfacets.

D, the GGX distribution, says how many of them face exactly along H.

Only a facet that faces along H reflects the light into the eye, so D sets how strong the highlight is.

On a smooth surface, almost all facets face the normal, so the lobe is tall and narrow.

${overCallout
    {' {sphereRoughnessRange 0.1 0.7}}
    "ggx-d"
    "On a rough surface, the facets spread out, so the lobe widens and drops."}

${cue "key" {' viewModeD}}
${cue "key" {'
    {motion {' {eased easingSine {orbit {' lightX lightY} {' -1.05 1.24} 60.0 sentence}}}}}}
${cue "key" {' {callout "ggx-d"}}}
Over the sphere, D is brightest where H meets the normal, and that spot follows the light.

### G: the facets that see both light and eye {#c4-g kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoSpecular panelValues 0.4 {'
    {vectors 1.0}
    {lobe 1.0}
    {paramFormula 1.0}
    {term 2}
    viewModeG
    {sphereRoughness 0.5}}}}
${cue "key" {' {motion {' {cameraPath brdfSweep}}}}}

${cue "key" {' panelCode listingSpecularGgx {highlightCode "specular-ggx" {' "g"}}}}
${cue "key" {' {callout "ggx-g"}}}
G removes the facets that their neighbours hide from the light or from the eye.

O3DE uses the height-correlated Smith form, which treats hiding from the light and hiding from the eye as related, not independent.

${overCallout
    {' {lightXRange -1.6 -2.5}}
    "ggx-g"
    "G itself falls at grazing angles, here as the light sinks toward the sphere's horizon."}

${cue "key" {' {lightX -1.6} {blend 1.5}}}
${cue "key" {' {callout "ggx-g"}}}
The microfacet formula divides by four times the product of N dot L and N dot V.

O3DE folds that divisor into G, so the code multiplies D by G and N dot L directly.

### F: Fresnel, from F zero toward white {#c4-f kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoSpecular panelValues 0.3 {'
    {vectors 1.0}
    {lobe 1.0}
    {paramFormula 1.0}
    {term 3}
    viewModeFinal
    {sphereRoughness 0.35}
    {sphereMetallic 0.0}}}}

${cue "key" {' panelCode listingSpecularGgx {highlightCode "specular-ggx" {' "f"}}}}
${cue "key" {' {callout "fresnel-schlick"}}}
F is Schlick's Fresnel, measured from the angle between H and the eye.

It is F zero when they line up, and it rises toward white as they move apart.

${overCallout
    {' {lightXRange -1.6 -2.6}}
    "fresnel-schlick"
    "As the light sinks toward the side, L moves away from V, so H turns away from V too, and F climbs."}

${cue "key" {' {lightX -2.28} {blend 2.0}}}
${cue "key" {' panelCode listingSpecularGgx {highlightCode "specular-ggx" {' "product"}}}}
The specular light is D times G times N dot L times F, times the light's intensity.

### Diffuse: Lambert, scaled by what reflection leaves {#c4-diffuse kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoDiffuse panelValues 0.4 {' {vectors 1.0} {lobe 0.0} {paramFormula 0.0}}}}
${cue "key" {' {motion {' {cameraPath brdfSweep}}}}}

${cue "key" {' panelCode listingDiffuseResponse {highlightCode "diffuse-response" {' "lambert"}}}}
${cue "key" {' viewModeNdotl}}
${cue "key" {'
    {motion {' {eased easingSine {orbit {' lightX lightY} {' -1.05 1.24} 70.0 sentence}}}}}}
${cue "key" {' {callout "diffuse-lambert"}}}
The diffuse light is Lambert's, the albedo divided by pi, times N dot L.

So it is brightest where the sphere faces the light.

${cue "key" {' viewModeDiffuseResponse}}
${cue "key" {' panelCode listingDiffuseResponse {highlightCode "diffuse-response" {' "response"}}}}
${cue "key" {' {callout "fresnel-roughness"}}}
The diffuse light is then scaled by the diffuse response, the share of light that is not reflected at the surface.

The response is one minus a Fresnel term at the view angle, so light that reflects off the surface does not also scatter diffusely.

${overCallout
    {' {sphereRoughnessRange 0.05 0.9}}
    "fresnel-roughness"
    "That Fresnel term is made gentler on rough surfaces, so the response stays near one minus F zero across the sphere."}

### Per light: (diffuse + specular) × intensity × shadow {#c4-perlight kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoPointlight panelValues 0.5 {' viewModeFinal}}}
${cue "key" {'
    {motion {' {cameraPath {' {pose 12.0 28.0 7.2} {pose -4.0 22.0 7.6} {pose 10.0 30.0 7.0}}}}}}}

${cue "key" {'
    panelCode
    listingSimplePointLight
    {highlightCode "simple-point-light" {' "falloff"}}}}
${cue "key" {' viewModeAttenuation}}
${overCallout
    {' {attenuationRadiusRange 2.0 6.0}}
    "point-light-apply"
    "For one simple point light, the shader first finds the falloff from the distance and the radius."}

${cue "key" {'
    panelCode
    listingSimplePointLight
    {highlightCode "simple-point-light" {' "intensity"}}}}
${cue "key" {' viewModeFinal {attenuationRadius 3.5} {blend 1.0}}}
${overCallout
    {' {lightIntensityRange 2.0 12.0}}
    "point-light-apply"
    "The intensity is the light's brightness in candelas, divided by the distance squared."}

It is then multiplied by the window, the factor from chapter two that falls to zero at the radius.

${cue "key" {' {lightIntensity 6.0} {blend 1.0}}}
${cue "key" {' {motion {' {path {' lightX} {' {' 1.4} {' -0.4}} sentence}}}}}
${cue "key" {' panelCode listingSimplePointLight {highlightCode "simple-point-light" {' "shade"}}}}
${cue "key" {' {callout "point-light-apply"}}}
Diffuse and specular are each multiplied by that intensity and by the shadow's lit ratio.

The lit ratio is the share of the light that no shadow blocks.

A simple point light casts no shadow, so its lit ratio is always one.

The results are added to the pixel's running sums of diffuse and specular light.

### Four lighting models, four BRDFs {#c4-models kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoSpecular panelValues 0.5 {' {vectors 0.0} {lobe 1.0} viewModeFinal}}}
${cue "key" {' {motion {' {cameraPath brdfSweep}}}}}

${cue "key" {' {callout "standard-brdf"}}}
Standard, the default model, is everything so far, Lambert's diffuse and GGX specular.

It also offers an optional clear coat.

${cue "key" {' diffuseModelNormalizedDisney}}
${cue "key" {' {callout "skin-diffuse"}}}
Skin uses a different diffuse, Disney's normalised diffuse, and so does Enhanced when subsurface scattering is on.

That diffuse brightens toward grazing angles on rough surfaces.

${cue "key" {' diffuseModelLambertian}}
${overCallout
    {' {sphereClearCoatRange 0.0 1.0}}
    "clear-coat-specular"
    "A clear coat adds a second, smooth GGX lobe, with its F zero fixed at zero point zero four."}

The coat also dims what lies beneath it, by the coat's own Fresnel.

${cue "key" {'
    {motion {' {eased easingSine {orbit {' lightX lightY} {' -1.05 1.24} 60.0 sentence}}}}}}
${cue "key" {' {callout "enhanced-brdf"}}}
Enhanced also offers anisotropic GGX, a lobe stretched along one direction of the surface.

Skin offers a second specular lobe, blended with the first.

Base is Standard without a clear coat.

${cue "key" {' {sphereClearCoat 0.0} {blend 1.0}}}
${cue "key" {' {callout "clear-coat-diffuse"}}}
The code shows that a coat dims direct diffuse light twice.

It dims that light once through the diffuse response, and once more inside each light's own calculation.

### The BRDF look-up texture, built once {#c4-lut kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoMultiscatter panelValues 0.4 {' {sphereRoughness 0.5} viewModeFinal}}}
${cue "key" {append teapotPoint {' {inspectObject 8.0} {pickShown 1.0}}}}
${cue "key" {' {motion {' {cameraPath brdfSweep}}}}}

${cue "key" {' {inset "lut"} imageFromBrdfLut imageCornerTopLeft {imageTitle "views.brdf_lut"}}}
${cue "key" {' {callout "brdf-execute-once"}}}
At start-up, before the first frame, a compute pass fills the BRDF look-up texture, two hundred and fifty-six texels on a side.

${cue "key" {' panelCode listingBrdfTexture {highlightCode "brdf-texture" {' "inputs"}}}}
${cue "key" {' {callout "brdf-texture"}}}
Across the texture runs N dot V, and down it runs one minus roughness.

Each texel averages the GGX reflection over the hemisphere, once for an F zero of zero and once for one.

$settle ${cue "key" {'
    {inset "lut"}
    {mark 0.0 0.0 0.5 1.0}
    {imageMarkText "views.mark_scale_a"}
    {blend 0.6}}} The view shows the texture's two channels side by side.

$settle On the left is the scale A, in red, the part of the reflection that grows with F zero.

$settle ${cue "key" {'
    {inset "lut"}
    {mark 0.5 0.0 1.0 1.0}
    {imageMarkText "views.mark_bias_b"}
    {blend 0.6}}} On the right is the bias B, in green, the part added whatever F zero is.

$settle The bias matters most at grazing angles.

$settle Across each half, N dot V runs from zero at the left, which is grazing, to one at the right, which is head on.

$settle ${cue "key" {'
    {inset "lut"}
    {mark 0.0 0.0 1.0 0.16}
    {imageMarkText "views.mark_rough"}
    {blend 0.6}}} Down each half, roughness runs from one at the top to zero at the bottom.

$settle The ring marks P's texel.

$settle That texel gives the two numbers that scale P's reflection of the sky.

${cue "key" {' {inset "lut"} unmark}}
${cue "key" {' panelCode listingBrdfTexture {highlightCode "brdf-texture" {' "sample"}}}}
${cue "key" {' {callout "brdf-texture"}}}
The pass averages one thousand and twenty-four directions per texel, chosen by importance sampling GGX.

${cue "key" {' panelCode listingBrdfTexture {highlightCode "brdf-texture" {' "store"}}}}
${cue "key" {' {callout "brdf-texture-size"}}}
That gives the two numbers per texel, the scale A and the bias B.

So the reflection over the whole hemisphere is F zero times A, plus B.

Because F zero sits outside the texture, one texture serves every material.

${overCallout
    {' {sphereRoughnessRange 0.2 0.8}}
    "brdf-texture"
    "The ringed texel is the one this point reads, and it moves up the texture as the sphere gets rougher."}

### Multiscatter: the energy single scattering loses {#c4-multi kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoMultiscatter panelValues 0.3 {'
    {sphereRoughness 0.2}
    viewModeFinal
    {multiscatter 0.0}}}}
${cue "key" {'
    {motion {' {cameraPath {' {pose 0.0 6.0 3.1} {pose -8.0 4.0 3.3} {pose 6.0 8.0 3.0}}}}}}}

${cue "key" {' panelCode listingMultiscatter {highlightCode "multiscatter" {' "energy"}}}}
${cue "key" {' {glide sphereRoughness 0.9 sentence}}}
${cue "key" {' {callout "multiscatter"}}}
A rough metal looks darker than it should.

GGX counts light that bounces once, and it loses the light that bounces between facets.

${overCallout
    {' {sphereRoughnessRange 0.2 0.95}}
    "multiscatter"
    "A plus B is the share that one bounce keeps, and it shrinks as roughness grows."}

${cue "key" {' panelCode listingMultiscatter {highlightCode "multiscatter" {' "compensate"}}}}
${cue "key" {' {multiscatter 1.0} {blend 1.5}}}
${cue "key" {' {callout "multiscatter"}}}
The compensation multiplies the specular light by a factor.

Take one divided by the kept share, which is A plus B, and subtract one.

That gives the light lost for each unit of light that one bounce keeps.

To get the factor, that loss is multiplied by F zero, and one is added.

${cue "key" {' viewModeMultiscatter}}
${overText
    {' {sphereRoughnessRange 0.2 0.95}}
    " The factor grows with roughness and with F zero, so rough metals gain the most."}

### Finding: compensation is off by default {#c4-default kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoMultiscatter panelValues 0.3 {'
    {sphereRoughness 0.8}
    viewModeFinal
    {multiscatter 1.0}}}}
${cue "key" {'
    {motion {' {cameraPath {' {pose 0.0 6.0 3.1} {pose -8.0 4.0 3.3} {pose 6.0 8.0 3.0}}}}}}}

${overCallout
    {' {multiscatterRange 0.0 1.0}}
    "ms-option"
    "Compensation has a shader option, and that option defaults to on."}

${cue "key" {' viewModeMultiscatter}}
${overCallout
    {' {multiscatterRange 1.0 0.0}}
    "ms-material-property"
    "But the material property that drives the option has no default value."}

So the property loads as false, and it switches the option off for every material that does not set it.

${cue "key" {' viewModeFinal}}
${cue "key" {' {callout "ms-preset"}}}
Only the metal presets and a few sample materials set the property.

The code shows that a default StandardPBR material is therefore rendered without compensation.

### One light, one formula {#c4-recap kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoSpecular panelValues 1.0 {'
    {vectors 1.0}
    {lobe 1.0}
    viewModeFinal
    {multiscatter 1.0}}}}
${cue "key" {'
    {motion {'
        {cameraPath {' {pose 0.0 6.0 3.1} {pose -12.0 4.0 3.3} {pose 8.0 8.0 3.0}}}
        {pingPonging {eased easingSine {orbit {' lightX lightY} {' -1.05 1.24} 60.0 beat}}}}}}}

So each light adds diffuse and specular light, weighed by D, G, F, and the diffuse response, all from the surface's numbers.

The look-up texture, made once at start-up, lets the later passes reuse that reflection for the whole sky.

$settle One factor in the formula is still open, the shadow's lit ratio.

$settle For the sun, that ratio is computed before the forward pass even starts.

### Where to read {#c4-sources kind=2d sources="specular-ggx, ggx-d, ggx-g, fresnel-schlick, diffuse-lambert, fresnel-roughness, point-light-apply, standard-brdf, skin-diffuse, clear-coat-specular, brdf-execute-once, brdf-texture, multiscatter, ms-material-property"}

These are the files and lines this chapter followed, in reading order.

## Shadows: a map from the sun, read through the screen {#c5 number=5 target=495 summary="The sun's cascaded shadow maps: four 1024-texel cascades split near-dense, each fitted with a sphere and snapped to texels, a point's lookup with its biases, the screen-space shadow buffer the forward pass reads, its blur, and the shadows of local lights." sources="cascade-image, cascade-splits, cascade-sphere-fit, cascade-snap, shadow-coords, shadow-normal-offset, cascade-select, shadow-is-shadowed, shadow-filter-default, fullscreen-shadow, fullscreen-shadow-normal, shadow-blur-settings, shadow-use-fullscreen, shadow-atlas, point-shadow-faces"}

### The sun's shadow is a depth map seen from the sun {#c5-why kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoCascades panelHidden 1.0 {' {labels 0.0}}}}
${cue "key" {'
    {motion {' {cameraPath {' {pose 28.0 26.0 7.5} {pose 8.0 20.0 8.2} {pose 24.0 30.0 7.2}}}}}}}

The sun's lit ratio comes from a shadow map, so this chapter follows how that map is made and read.

${cue "key" {' {callout "one-shadow-light"}}} O3DE shadows a single directional light.

It draws the scene's depth, as seen from that light, into a shadow map.

${cue "key" {'
    {inset "cm"}
    imageFromCascadeMap
    {imageCascade 4.0}
    {imageParamFrom 0.9}
    imageCornerBottomLeft
    {imageTitle "views.cascade_map"}}}
${cue "key" {' {callout "cascade-image"}}}
The map is one texture array of thirty-two bit float depth.

It has ${spoken {cascadeCount}} slices of one thousand and twenty-four by one thousand and twenty-four texels.

Each slice belongs to one cascade, a shadow map for one range of distances from the camera.

$settle ${cue "key" {'
    {inset "cm"}
    {mark 0.0 0.0 0.25 1.0}
    {imageMarkText "views.mark_cascade_0"}
    {blend 0.6}}} The view lays the four slices side by side, nearest cascade first, and tints each with its debug colour.

$settle Each texel holds the depth, from the sun, of the first surface the sun meets there.

$settle Bright is near the sun and dark is far, over the depth range set for this view.

$settle ${cue "key" {'
    {inset "cm"}
    {mark 0.5 0.0 1.0 1.0}
    {imageMarkText "views.mark_whole_room"}
    {blend 0.6}}} The far cascades cover much more ground.

$settle So the whole room fills a sliver of their depth, and it reads as one flat bright patch.

$settle P is ringed in each slice that covers it.

$settle The shadow test compares P's own depth from the sun with the depth stored under that ring.

${cue "key" {' {inset "cm"} unmark}}
${cue "key" {' {glide sunYaw 75.0 sentence}}}
${cue "key" {' {callout "shadow-caster-state"}}}
Every shadow-casting mesh is drawn into the map with only a depth test and a small depth bias.

This happens every frame, so the cascades follow the camera and the sun.

### Four cascades, split near-dense {#c5-cascades kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoCascades panelValues 0.5 {'
    {labels 0.0}
    {freeCameraOn 1.0}
    {cascades 1.0}
    {cascadesShown 4.0}
    {shadowFar 30.0}
    {sunYaw 35.0}}}}
${cue "key" {'
    {freeCameraTarget {' 0.0 0.0 0.0}}
    {freeCameraDistance 26.0}
    {freeCameraPitch 48.0}
    {freeCameraYaw 36.0}
    {cameraTarget {' -1.05 0.9 -0.55}}
    {cameraYaw 49.6}
    {cameraPitch 9.0}
    {cameraDistance 4.45}
    {cameraFov 60.0}}}

${cue "key" {' {callout "cascade-splits"}}}
The cascades cut the camera's view into four slices of depth.

Each gets the same number of texels, so the near ones are small and sharp, and the far ones wide and coarse.

${cue "key" {' {callout "cascade-defaults"}}}
By default there are four cascades, reaching out to a shadow far distance of one hundred metres.

Here that distance is shortened to thirty metres, so all four cascades fit in view.

${overCallout
    {' {cascadeSplitRatioRange 0.0 1.0}}
    "cascade-splits"
    "Each cut point between cascades blends a uniform spacing with a logarithmic one, by a ratio."}

A ratio of zero is uniform, one is logarithmic, and the default is zero point nine.

A logarithmic split packs the cascades close to the camera.

${cue "key" {' {cascadeSplitRatio 0.9} {blend 1.5}}}
${cue "key" {' {callout "cascade-splits"}}}
This camera's near plane is zero point one metres.

With the default ratio, these thirty metres split at about one point one, three point one, and eight point seven metres.

The full hundred metres would split at three, seven point nine, and twenty-three point five.

${cue "key" {'
    {inset "cm"}
    imageFromCascadeMap
    {imageCascade 4.0}
    {imageParamFrom 0.9}
    imageCornerBottomLeft
    {imageTitle "views.cascade_map"}}}
${cue "key" {' {cascadeColors 1.0}}}
${cue "key" {' {callout "cascade-select"}}}
O3DE's debug colouring tints each pixel by its cascade, red, green, blue, and then yellow.

### A sphere around each slice, snapped to texels {#c5-fit kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoCascades panelValues 0.4 {'
    {labels 0.0}
    {freeCameraOn 1.0}
    {cascades 1.0}
    {cascadesShown 4.0}
    {shadowFar 30.0}
    {cascadeSpheres 0.0}
    {cascadeBoxes 0.0}
    {cascadeColors 0.0}}}}
${cue "key" {'
    {freeCameraTarget {' 0.0 0.0 0.0}}
    {freeCameraDistance 26.0}
    {freeCameraPitch 48.0}
    {freeCameraYaw 36.0}
    {cameraTarget {' -1.05 0.9 -0.55}}
    {cameraYaw 49.6}
    {cameraPitch 9.0}
    {cameraDistance 4.45}
    {cameraFov 60.0}}}

${overCallout
    {' {cascadeSpheresRange 0.0 1.0}}
    "cascade-sphere-fit"
    "Each slice is wrapped in a bounding sphere, so its size does not change as the camera turns."}

${overCallout
    {' {cascadeBoxesRange 0.0 1.0}}
    "cascade-sphere-fit"
    "In the light's view, the cascade's box is the cube around that sphere, stretched back toward the sun."}

So casters between the sun and the view still land in the map, even when they are behind the camera.

${cue "key" {' {motion {' {path {' cameraYaw} {' {' 74.6} {' 24.6} {' 49.6}} sentence}}}}}
${cue "key" {' {callout "cascade-snap"}}}
The box is snapped to whole shadow-map texels.

So as the camera moves, the map's texels stay fixed in the world, and shadow edges do not shimmer.

${cue "key" {' {callout "cascade-sphere-fit"}}}
A cascade's texel is its sphere's diameter over one thousand and twenty-four, so the far cascades are much coarser.

### P in its cascade: offset, biased, compared {#c5-lookup kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoCascades panelHidden 0.4 {' {labels 0.0} {shadowFar 100.0}}}}
${cue "key" {append teapotPoint {' {inspectObject 8.0} {pickShown 1.0}}}}
${cue "key" {'
    {motion {' {cameraPath {' {pose 20.0 22.0 5.0} {pose 6.0 18.0 5.4} {pose 18.0 24.0 4.8}}}}}}}

${cue "key" {'
    panelCode
    listingFullscreenShadow
    {highlightCode "fullscreen-shadow" {' "position"}}}}
${cue "key" {' {callout "fullscreen-shadow"}}}
To shade P, the shadow pass first rebuilds P's position from the depth.

${cue "key" {' panelCode listingFullscreenShadow {highlightCode "fullscreen-shadow" {' "offset"}}}}
${cue "key" {' {callout "shadow-normal-offset"}}}
It then moves P a little along its normal.

The size of this normal offset is the normal bias setting times two, divided by the map's size in texels.

With the default bias of two point five, that is two point five times two over one thousand and twenty-four, about five millimetres.

${cue "key" {' panelCode listingFullscreenShadow {highlightCode "fullscreen-shadow" {' "range"}}}}
${cue "key" {'
    {inset "cm"}
    imageFromCascadeMap
    {imageCascade 4.0}
    {imageParamFrom 0.9}
    imageCornerBottomLeft
    {imageTitle "views.cascade_map"}}}
${cue "key" {' {callout "cascade-select"}}}
The pass takes the first cascade whose map holds P at least one and a half texels in from the edge.

${cue "key" {' panelCode listingFullscreenShadow {highlightCode "fullscreen-shadow" {' "compare"}}}}
${cue "key" {' {callout "shadow-coords"}}}
P's depth from the sun is moved a millimetre and a half toward the sun.

That depth is then compared with the one stored at P's texel.

${cue "key" {' {callout "shadow-is-shadowed"}}}
If something nearer the sun was stored there, P is in shadow.

O3DE can filter this compare over nearby texels, but the default filter is None, so the answer is exactly zero or one.

### Biases against acne; the slope bias is off {#c5-bias kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoCascades panelHidden 0.3 {'
    {labels 0.0}
    {shadowFar 100.0}
    {normalShadowBias 2.5}}}}
${cue "key" {'
    {cameraTarget {' 0.8 0.0 0.8}}
    {cameraDistance 3.2}
    {cameraPitch 40.0}
    {cameraYaw 30.0}}}

${cue "key" {'
    {inset "sb"}
    imageFromShadowBuffer
    {imageBlurred 0.0}
    imageCornerTopLeft
    {imageTitle "views.shadow_buffer"}}}
${cue "key" {' {callout "shadow-caster-state"}}}
Without a bias, a surface can shadow itself in a pattern of stripes, called acne.

$settle ${cue "key" {'
    {inset "sb"}
    {mark 0.62 0.08 1.0 0.62}
    {imageMarkText "views.mark_lit"}
    {blend 0.6}}} The shadow buffer is a screen-sized texture that holds each pixel's result in one byte.

$settle It is white where the sun is seen.

$settle ${cue "key" {'
    {inset "sb"}
    {mark 0.0 0.0 0.5 0.42}
    {imageMarkText "views.mark_shadow"}
    {blend 0.6}}} It is black where the sun is hidden.

$settle Each pixel holds one test against the cascade, before the blur that comes later, so the shadow's edges follow the shadow map's texels.

$settle Acne would show here as black stripes across faces the sun lights, where a surface fails the test against itself.

${cue "key" {' {inset "sb"} unmark}}
${overCallout
    {' {normalShadowBiasRange 0.0 6.0}}
    "shadow-normal-offset"
    "The normal offset lifts the lookup off the surface, and a larger offset lets shadows detach from their casters' feet."}

${cue "key" {' {normalShadowBias 2.5} {blend 1.5}}}
${cue "key" {' {callout "shadow-slope-bias-off"}}}
A slope bias, which grows as a surface tilts away from the sun, is computed for each cascade and uploaded.

But the shader's switch for it is off, so that bias never acts.

${cue "key" {' {callout "shadow-normal-offset"}}}
The code shows that the normal offset depends only on the map's size, not on the cascade.

So a far cascade, with texels many times coarser, gets the same five millimetres as the nearest one.

### Opaque pixels read a shadow buffer, not the cascades {#c5-fullscreen kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoCascades panelHidden 0.6 {' {labels 0.0} {shadowFar 100.0}}}}
${cue "key" {append teapotPoint {' {inspectObject 8.0} {pickShown 1.0}}}}
${cue "key" {'
    {motion {' {cameraPath {' {pose 28.0 26.0 7.5} {pose 10.0 20.0 8.0} {pose 26.0 28.0 7.2}}}}}}}

${cue "key" {'
    {inset "sb"}
    imageFromShadowBuffer
    {imageBlurred 0.0}
    imageCornerTopLeft
    {imageTitle "views.shadow_buffer"}}}
${cue "key" {' {callout "fullscreen-shadow"}}}
All of this runs in a full-screen pass, once per pixel, before the forward pass.

Its result is the shadow buffer, one eight bit shadow value per pixel.

${cue "key" {' {callout "fullscreen-shadow-normal"}}}
The normal that this pass offsets along is not the mesh's normal.

The pass rebuilds it from the depths of the pixel's neighbours, so it is the normal of the flat face.

${cue "key" {' {callout "shadow-use-fullscreen"}}}
Every opaque and alpha cut-out material reads this buffer in the forward pass.

Transparent materials look up the cascades themselves.

### A binary shadow, softened in screen space {#c5-blur kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoCascades panelHidden 0.3 {'
    {labels 0.0}
    {shadowFar 100.0}
    {shadowBlur 0.0}}}}
${cue "key" {'
    {cameraTarget {' -0.6 0.2 -0.4}}
    {cameraDistance 3.4}
    {cameraPitch 34.0}
    {cameraYaw 24.0}}}

${cue "key" {'
    {inset "raw"}
    imageFromShadowBuffer
    {imageBlurred 0.0}
    imageCornerTopLeft
    {imageTitle "views.shadow_buffer"}}}
${cue "key" {' {callout "shadow-filter-default"}}}
The default filter is None, so the raw buffer holds hard zeros and ones, stepped at the cascade's texels.

${cue "key" {'
    {inset "blur"}
    imageFromShadowBuffer
    {imageBlurred 1.0}
    imageCornerTopLeft
    {imageTitle "views.shadow_buffer"}}}
${overCallout
    {' {shadowBlurRange 0.0 1.0}}
    "shadow-blur-settings"
    "A depth-aware blur then runs over the buffer, first vertically and then horizontally."}

It reaches eight pixels to each side, with a constant falloff setting of two thirds.

$settle ${cue "key" {'
    {inset "raw"}
    {mark 0.3 0.2 0.72 0.62}
    {imageMarkText "views.mark_stepped"}
    {blend 0.6}}} The left view is the raw buffer, with hard zeros and ones and edges stepped at the cascade's texels.

$settle ${cue "key" {'
    {inset "blur"}
    {mark 0.3 0.2 0.72 0.62}
    {imageMarkText "views.mark_softened"}
    {blend 0.6}}} The right view shows the buffer after the blur, with the same edges softened into greys.

$settle A grey pixel is now partly in shadow, and the forward pass multiplies the sun's light by that fraction.

${cue "key" {' {inset "raw"} unmark}}
${cue "key" {' {inset "blur"} unmark}}
${cue "key" {' {callout "shadow-blur-settings"}}}
Where the depth's slope changes, at an object's edge, the blur stops mixing, so a shadow does not bleed onto a surface behind it.

${cue "key" {' {callout "pcf-squared"}}}
Two softer filters exist as options, percentage-closer filtering and exponential shadow maps, but the lab does not port them.

Percentage-closer filtering, or PCF, averages the compare over nearby texels.

The code shows that the PCF kernels square their result.

### Spot and point shadows live in an atlas {#c5-local kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoCascades panelHidden 0.8 {' {labels 0.0} {shadowFar 100.0}}}}
${cue "key" {'
    {motion {' {cameraPath {' {pose 28.0 26.0 7.5} {pose 12.0 22.0 7.8} {pose 24.0 28.0 7.2}}}}}}}

${cue "key" {' {callout "shadow-atlas"}}}
Spot, disk, and sphere lights can cast shadows too, but their shadows are off by default.

Their maps share one atlas, a large texture that holds many maps side by side.

${cue "key" {' {callout "point-shadow-faces"}}}
A sphere light needs six maps, one per face of a cube around it.

Each map has a ninety-one degree field of view, so the faces overlap at their seams.

${cue "key" {' {callout "shadow-atlas"}}}
Maps of different sizes are packed into the atlas like a quadtree, each at least two hundred and fifty-six texels on a side.

### The shadow, ready before the light {#c5-recap kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoCascades panelHidden 1.0 {' {labels 0.0} {shadowFar 100.0}}}}
${cue "key" {'
    {motion {'
        {cameraPath {' {pose 28.0 26.0 7.5} {pose 8.0 20.0 8.2} {pose 24.0 30.0 7.2}}}
        {path {' sunYaw} {' {' 55.0} {' 30.0}} beat}}}}}

${cue "key" {'
    {inset "blur"}
    imageFromShadowBuffer
    {imageBlurred 1.0}
    imageCornerTopLeft
    {imageTitle "views.shadow_buffer"}}}
So the sun's shadow is computed once per pixel, from four cascades, before any light is shaded.

The forward pass only reads one number per pixel, and the blur makes a hard compare look soft.

$settle The direct light is now complete.

The rest of a pixel's light comes from everything around it, and the forward pass left that light out.

### Where to read {#c5-sources kind=2d sources="cascade-image, shadow-caster-state, cascade-splits, cascade-defaults, cascade-sphere-fit, cascade-snap, fullscreen-shadow, shadow-normal-offset, cascade-select, shadow-coords, shadow-is-shadowed, shadow-slope-bias-off, fullscreen-shadow-normal, shadow-blur-settings, shadow-use-fullscreen, shadow-atlas, point-shadow-faces"}

These are the files and lines this chapter followed, in reading order.

## Indirect light: the sky and the probes {#c6 number=6 target=435 summary="Image-based lighting from the global skylight's two cubemaps (cosine-convolved diffuse, GGX-prefiltered specular with mip = 5 x roughness), the split sum, reflection probes with their stencil, weights, and parallax correction, and, in passing, SSR and the diffuse probe grid." sources="diffuse-global-stencil, probe-bake-capture, probe-defaults, reflection-clear, ibl-srg, ibl-diffuse-lookup, diffuse-global, diffuse-composite, specular-preset, cubemap-ggx, roughness-mip, reflection-global, probe-stencil, probe-blend-weight, parallax-obb, probe-render-inner, ssr-default-off"}

### Two cubemaps: one for diffuse, one for specular {#c6-two kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoIbl panelHidden 0.6 {' {labels 0.0}}}}
${cue "key" {'
    {motion {' {cameraPath {' {pose 20.0 10.0 3.6} {pose 4.0 6.0 3.9} {pose 18.0 14.0 3.4}}}}}}}

The sky's light reaches the frame through two cubemaps, made from the sky image when the assets are built.

${cue "key" {'
    {inset "spec"}
    imageFromCubemap
    {imageMip 0.0}
    imageCornerTopLeft
    {imageTitle "views.cubemap_specular"}}}
${cue "key" {' {inset "spec"} {imageMip 5.0} {blend 6.0}}}
${cue "key" {' {callout "specular-preset"}}}
The specular cubemap has six mips, with two hundred and fifty-six texels a side at the top by default.

Each smaller mip is prefiltered for a rougher surface.

${cue "key" {'
    {inset "diff"}
    imageFromCubemap
    {imageDiffuse 1.0}
    imageCornerTopRight
    {imageTitle "views.cubemap_diffuse"}}}
${cue "key" {' {callout "diffuse-preset"}}}
The diffuse cubemap is the sky convolved with a cosine over the whole hemisphere.

Each of its texels holds the average light arriving at one normal, and it has one small mip.

$settle ${cue "key" {'
    {inset "spec"}
    {mark 0.25 0.0 0.5 0.34}
    {imageMarkText "views.mark_up_face"}
    {blend 0.6}}} Each cube is unfolded into a cross, with the face that looks up on top.

$settle ${cue "key" {'
    {inset "spec"}
    {mark 0.0 0.33 1.0 0.67}
    {imageMarkText "views.mark_sides"}
    {blend 0.6}}} The four faces that look around the horizon sit in a row, and they show the trees and the field.

$settle ${cue "key" {'
    {inset "spec"}
    {mark 0.25 0.66 0.5 1.0}
    {imageMarkText "views.mark_down_face"}
    {blend 0.6}}} The face looking down is below it.

$settle A direction from the cube's centre picks a point on one face, and every lookup reads the cube that way.

$settle The specular cube on the left shows its sharpest mip.

$settle The diffuse cube on the right is the same sky averaged over half a sphere, so it is almost flat.

${cue "key" {' {inset "spec"} unmark}}
${overCallout
    {' {iblExposureRange -2.0 1.0}}
    "ibl-srg"
    "Both cubes are bound for the whole scene, with one exposure and one orientation."}

The lab builds them from its own sky, at the same sizes.

### Diffuse IBL: one lookup by the normal {#c6-diffuse kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoRoom panelHidden 0.6 {append baseLight {'
    {labels 0.0}
    {forwardStage 0.0}}}}}
${cue "key" {append teapotPoint {' {inspectObject 8.0} {pickShown 1.0}}}}
${cue "key" {'
    {motion {' {cameraPath {' {pose 18.0 18.0 5.8} {pose 2.0 14.0 6.2} {pose 16.0 20.0 5.6}}}}}}}

${cue "key" {'
    {inset "diff"}
    imageFromCubemap
    {imageDiffuse 1.0}
    imageCornerTopLeft
    {imageTitle "views.cubemap_diffuse"}}}
${cue "key" {' {callout "ibl-diffuse-lookup"}}}
For P, diffuse image-based light is a single lookup of that cubemap along P's normal.

The ring in the diffuse cross marks that direction.

${overCallout
    {' {forwardStageRange 0.0 1.0}}
    "diffuse-global"
    "A full-screen pass multiplies that light by the albedo target, which already holds the diffuse response and occlusion."}

It adds the result to the diffuse target.

${cue "key" {chainTarget "st" 6.0 imageCornerTopRight {' {imageTitle "views.stencil"}}}}
${cue "key" {' {callout "diffuse-global-stencil"}}}
The pass runs only on pixels whose stencil has the top bit, one hundred and twenty-eight, from chapter one.

${cue "key" {' {callout "diffuse-composite"}}}
Projects made from O3DE's default template replace that pass with a gem, O3DE's name for an optional module.

That gem is the diffuse probe grid, which lights rooms from a grid of light probes.

The gem's own pass adds the grid's light, and it applies the same sky light wherever no probe grid covers the pixel.

### Specular IBL: the reflection, a mip chosen by roughness {#c6-specular kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoIbl panelValues 0.4 {' {labels 0.0} {sphereRoughness 0.05}}}}
${cue "key" {'
    {inspectX -1.05}
    {inspectY 1.24}
    {inspectZ -0.55}
    {inspectNX 0.0}
    {inspectNY 1.0}
    {inspectNZ 0.0}
    {inspectObject 5.0}
    {pickShown 1.0}}}
${cue "key" {'
    {motion {' {cameraPath {' {pose 20.0 10.0 3.6} {pose 6.0 8.0 3.8} {pose 18.0 12.0 3.5}}}}}}}

${cue "key" {'
    {inset "spec"}
    imageFromCubemap
    {imageMip -1.0}
    imageCornerTopLeft
    {imageTitle "views.cubemap_specular"}}}
${cue "key" {' {callout "reflection-global"}}}
For specular, the pass reflects the view about the normal, and looks up the specular cubemap along that direction.

The specular cross now shows the mip that P reads.

The ring marks where P's reflected ray points, and the mip blurs as P's roughness rises.

${overCallout
    {' {sphereRoughnessRange 0.05 0.95}}
    "roughness-mip"
    "The pass reads at mip level roughness times five, blending the two nearest mips, so a rougher surface reads a blurrier sky."}

${cue "key" {' {callout "cubemap-ggx"}}}
Each mip m was prefiltered with the GGX lobe of roughness m over five, using two hundred and fifty-six samples per texel.

So the mip a surface reads matches its own lobe.

${cue "key" {' {sphereRoughness 0.3} {blend 1.5}}}
The ringed area is the sky that this point of the sphere reflects.

### The split sum: the cube times F zero A plus B {#c6-splitsum kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoIbl panelValues 0.4 {' {labels 0.0} {sphereRoughness 0.3}}}}
${cue "key" {'
    {inspectX -1.05}
    {inspectY 1.24}
    {inspectZ -0.55}
    {inspectNX 0.0}
    {inspectNY 1.0}
    {inspectNZ 0.0}
    {inspectObject 5.0}
    {pickShown 1.0}}}
${cue "key" {'
    {motion {' {cameraPath {' {pose 20.0 10.0 3.6} {pose 6.0 8.0 3.8} {pose 18.0 12.0 3.5}}}}}}}

${cue "key" {' {inset "lut"} imageFromBrdfLut imageCornerTopLeft {imageTitle "views.brdf_lut"}}}
${overCallout
    {' {sphereRoughnessRange 0.1 0.8}}
    "reflection-global"
    "The prefiltered colour is then multiplied by the reflection factor of chapter four, F zero times A, plus B."}

In the look-up view, the ring marks P's texel, whose red is A and whose green is B.

${cue "key" {' {callout "reflection-global"}}}
This is the split sum, which splits an integral over the hemisphere into two pieces computed ahead of time.

The light is in the cube, and the reflection's response is in the texture.

${overCallout
    {' {iblExposureRange 0.0 1.0}}
    "reflection-global-weight"
    "The result is scaled by two to the power of the sky's exposure setting."}

The reflection is also scaled by the specular occlusion, from the fourth channel of the albedo target.

### A reflection probe: a local cube for a box of space {#c6-probe kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoReflections panelHidden 0.6 {' {labels 0.0}}}}
${cue "key" {'
    {motion {' {cameraPath {' {pose 18.0 20.0 6.4} {pose 4.0 14.0 6.8} {pose 16.0 22.0 6.2}}}}}}}

The sky's cubemap was made outside the room, so the floor reflects a sky it should not see.

A reflection probe fixes that.

${cue "key" {' {probeCubemap 0.0}}}
${overCallout
    {' {probeCubemapRange 0.0 1.0}}
    "probe-bake-capture"
    "A probe renders the room around it into its own cubemap, then prefilters it like the sky's."}

${cue "key" {' {probeCubemap 0.0} {blend 1.0}}}
${overCallout
    {' {probeBoxesRange 0.0 1.0}}
    "probe-defaults"
    "Each probe has an outer box, where its reflection fades in, and an inner box, where it replaces everything else."}

${cue "key" {' viewModeProbeStencil}}
${cue "key" {' {callout "probe-stencil"}}}
A stencil pass counts how many inner boxes hold each pixel's surface.

The count starts from three, because a mesh writes three into the two low bits, as chapter one showed.

Each inner box that holds the surface adds one, so later passes know how deeply each pixel's probes are nested.

${over
    {' {probeInnerScaleRange 0.2 0.9}}
    {cat {'
        {text " "}
        {cue "key" {' viewModeProbeWeight}}
        {text " "}
        {cue "key" {' {callout "probe-blend-weight"}}}
        {text
            " Between the boxes, a weight ramps from zero at the outer box to one at the inner box."}}}}

The weights of all probes add up in a target of their own.

### Parallax correction: aim where the ray meets the box {#c6-parallax kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoReflections panelHidden 0.4 {'
    {labels 0.0}
    viewModeFinal
    {probeParallax 0.0}}}}
${cue "key" {'
    {motion {' {cameraPath {' {pose 18.0 20.0 6.4} {pose 6.0 16.0 6.7} {pose 16.0 22.0 6.2}}}}}}}

${cue "key" {' {callout "parallax-obb"}}} A cubemap holds the room as seen from one point.

So a reflection read straight from it lands in the wrong place, on any part of the floor far from that point.

${overCallout
    {' {probeParallaxRange 0.0 1.0}}
    "parallax-obb"
    "Parallax correction traces the reflected ray out to the point where it hits the probe's outer box."}

The lookup then aims at that hit point, so the reflections slide into line with the room.

${overCallout
    {' {floorRoughnessRange 0.05 0.4}}
    "parallax-obb"
    "The correction assumes that the room is the box and the probe sits at its centre."}

So the correction is exact only for walls that lie on the faces of the box.

### Probes and the sky blend by weight {#c6-blend kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoReflections panelValues 0.4 {'
    {labels 0.0}
    viewModeFinal
    {probeParallax 1.0}}}}
${cue "key" {'
    {motion {' {cameraPath {' {pose 18.0 20.0 6.4} {pose 6.0 16.0 6.7} {pose 16.0 22.0 6.2}}}}}}}

${overCallout
    {' {probeInnerScaleRange 0.9 0.2}}
    "reflection-global-weight"
    "The sky's reflection is drawn first, weighted by one minus the probes' total weight."}

${cue "key" {' {callout "probe-render-outer"}}}
Each probe then adds its own reflection, and where probes overlap, each probe's weight is divided by the sum of all their weights.

${cue "key" {' {probeInnerScale 0.55} {blend 1.5}}}
${cue "key" {' {callout "probe-render-inner"}}}
Inside an inner box, the probe overwrites the result at full weight.

With nested probes, the smallest is drawn last, so it wins.

### Read from the code: what the probes leave out {#c6-findings kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoReflections panelHidden 0.6 {' {labels 0.0} viewModeFinal}}}
${cue "key" {'
    {motion {' {cameraPath {' {pose 18.0 20.0 6.4} {pose 2.0 14.0 6.8} {pose 16.0 22.0 6.2}}}}}}}

${cue "key" {' {callout "probe-render-outer"}}}
The code shows that the probe passes are not given the albedo target.

So the specular occlusion in the albedo target's fourth channel never reaches a probe's reflection, and only the sky's reflection is occluded.

${cue "key" {' {callout "reflection-clear"}}}
The code also clears the reflection target to a grey of zero point four.

Some pixels inside an inner box have image-based light switched off.

Such a pixel keeps the grey, and the grey is added to its specular.

Both findings come from reading the code, not from running the engine.

### Two more sources, off or out of reach {#c6-ssr-dpg kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoRoom panelHidden 1.0 {append baseLight {'
    {labels 0.0}
    {forwardStage 3.0}}}}}
${cue "key" {'
    {motion {' {cameraPath {' {pose 20.0 20.0 6.2} {pose 0.0 14.0 6.6} {pose 16.0 18.0 6.0}}}}}}}

${cue "key" {' {callout "ssr-default-off"}}}
Screen-space reflections, which trace rays against the depth buffer, also exist.

They trace at half resolution, with ninety-six steps per ray.

But they are off unless a level adds the component that enables them.

${cue "key" {' {callout "diffuse-composite"}}}
The diffuse probe grid traces rays from probes to give rooms bounced diffuse light.

Its update shaders ship only as compiled binaries, so only the pass that adds its light can be read.

### Light from everything around {#c6-recap kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoIbl panelHidden 1.0 {' {labels 0.0} {sphereRoughness 0.3}}}}
${cue "key" {'
    {motion {'
        {cameraPath {' {pose 20.0 10.0 3.6} {pose 0.0 6.0 3.9} {pose 16.0 14.0 3.4}}}
        {path {' sphereRoughness} {' {' 0.7} {' 0.15}} beat}}}}}

${cue "key" {'
    {inset "spec"}
    imageFromCubemap
    {imageMip -1.0}
    imageCornerTopLeft
    {imageTitle "views.cubemap_specular"}}}
So the sky's light is prefiltered once, into cubes and a texture, and each pixel then pays for only two lookups and a multiply.

Probes replace the sky where a room needs its own reflection.

They are blended by weights that come from the stencil and the probes' boxes.

$settle Those lookups assume that nothing blocks the sky.

Near the floor, something does, and that is the next part.

### Where to read {#c6-sources kind=2d sources="ibl-srg, specular-preset, diffuse-preset, ibl-diffuse-lookup, diffuse-global, diffuse-composite, reflection-global, roughness-mip, cubemap-ggx, reflection-global-weight, probe-stencil, probe-blend-weight, parallax-obb, probe-render-outer, probe-render-inner, ssr-default-off"}

These are the files and lines this chapter followed, in reading order.

## Occlusion: what the sky cannot reach {#c7 number=7 target=325 summary="Screen-space ambient occlusion from the depth buffer alone: 52 samples per half-resolution pixel, a radius set as a fraction of the screen, a gamma of 2.2, a depth-aware blur and upsample, and a multiply into all diffuse light; and the material's own occlusion maps." sources="ssao-defaults, ssao-default, ssao-order, ssao-compute, ssao-normal, ssao-view-position, ssao-local-samples, ssao-accumulate, ssao-radius, ssao-format, depth-upsample, ssao-modulate, occlusion-input, occlusion-finalize"}

### SSAO darkens creases from depth alone {#c7-why kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoSsao panelHidden 0.6 {' {labels 0.0} viewModeFinal {ssao 0.0}}}}
${cue "key" {append teapotPoint {' {inspectObject 8.0} {pickShown 1.0}}}}
${cue "key" {'
    {cameraTarget {' -0.25 0.15 -1.55}}
    {cameraDistance 1.5}
    {cameraPitch 24.0}
    {cameraYaw -28.0}}}

${cue "key" {'
    {motion {'
        {path
            {' cameraYaw cameraPitch cameraDistance}
            {' {' 30.0 18.0 2.4} {' 8.0 26.0 2.8}}
            sentence}}}}}
Image-based light assumes that the whole sky is visible.

But near the teapot's foot, the floor and the pot hide much of the sky.

${overCallout
    {' {ssaoRange 0.0 1.0}}
    "ssao-default"
    "Screen-space ambient occlusion, or SSAO, is on by default."}

It darkens where nearby geometry hides the sky, and it uses nothing but the depth buffer.

${cue "key" {'
    {inset "raw"}
    imageFromSsaoStage
    {imageStage 1.0}
    imageCornerTopLeft
    {imageTitle "views.ssao_stage"}}}
${cue "key" {' {callout "ssao-order"}}}
SSAO runs after the forward pass and the sky's light.

It reads a half-resolution copy of the linear depth, and writes an eight bit target.

$settle ${cue "key" {'
    {inset "raw"}
    {mark 0.0 0.5 0.42 1.0}
    {imageMarkText "views.mark_unoccluded"}
    {blend 0.6}}} The view shows that target, one byte per pixel at half resolution, and it is white where nothing occludes.

$settle ${cue "key" {'
    {inset "raw"}
    {mark 0.3 0.22 1.0 0.48}
    {imageMarkText "views.mark_crease"}
    {blend 0.6}}} It is darker in the creases, like where the wall meets the floor.

$settle There, many of the depth samples around a pixel land on the other surface, above the pixel's own surface.

$settle The darker a pixel, the less of the sky's diffuse light it keeps.

### Fifty-two samples around P, weighted by distance {#c7-samples kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoSsao panelValues 0.3 {'
    {labels 0.0}
    viewModeFinal
    {ssao 1.0}
    {ssaoSamples 0.0}}}}
${cue "key" {'
    {inspectX -0.25}
    {inspectY 0.0}
    {inspectZ -1.4}
    {inspectNX 0.0}
    {inspectNY 1.0}
    {inspectNZ 0.0}
    {inspectObject 0.0}
    {pickShown 1.0}}}
${cue "key" {'
    {cameraTarget {' -0.3 0.2 -1.55}}
    {cameraDistance 1.6}
    {cameraPitch 26.0}
    {cameraYaw 22.0}}}

${cue "key" {' {glide cameraYaw 34.0 sentence}}}
${cue "key" {' {callout "ssao-view-position"}}}
We follow a floor point at the teapot's foot.

For that point's half-resolution pixel, the shader rebuilds the position in view space from the depth.

${cue "key" {chainTarget "n" 4.0 imageCornerTopLeft {'
    {imageChannel 5.0}
    {imageTitle "views.target_normal"}}}}
${cue "key" {' {callout "ssao-normal"}}}
Its normal is rebuilt from the neighbours' depths too.

On each axis, the shader looks at the two neighbours, and uses the one whose depth is closer to the point's.

So the normal does not jump across an edge.

Because the normal comes from depth, normal maps never reach SSAO.

The normal view reads as in chapter one, with colour as direction, green up and blue toward the camera.

${over
    {' {ssaoSamplesRange 0.0 12.0}}
    {cat {'
        {text " "}
        {cue "key" {' {imageHide {' "n"}}}}
        {text " "}
        {cue "key" {' {callout "ssao-local-samples"}}}
        {text
            " First come twelve fixed samples, four and eight pixels away, in a cross and its diagonals."}}}}

${overCallout
    {' {ssaoSamplesRange 12.0 52.0}}
    "ssao-compute"
    "Then forty more samples follow along a spiral, in ten rounds of four."}

The spiral is shared between the sixteen pixels of a four by four block.

${cue "key" {'
    {motion {' {path {' cameraYaw cameraPitch} {' {' 40.0 14.0} {' 10.0 30.0}} sentence}}}}}
${cue "key" {' {callout "ssao-accumulate"}}}
Each sample counts by how far it rises above the point's surface.

That rise is measured as the cosine between the normal and the direction to the sample.

The sample's weight is one over the sum of its squared distance and zero point zero seven.

So samples on the teapot occlude the point, and samples on the floor do not.

${cue "key" {' {callout "ssao-accumulate"}}} The occlusion is the weighted average of those cosines.

No test drops a sample for being too far away, so a far sample fades only through its weight.

### The radius is a share of the screen, not metres {#c7-radius kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoSsao panelValues 0.3 {'
    {labels 0.0}
    viewModeFinal
    {ssao 1.0}
    {ssaoSamples 52.0}}}}
${cue "key" {'
    {inspectX -0.25}
    {inspectY 0.0}
    {inspectZ -1.4}
    {inspectNX 0.0}
    {inspectNY 1.0}
    {inspectNZ 0.0}
    {inspectObject 0.0}
    {pickShown 1.0}}}
${cue "key" {'
    {cameraTarget {' -0.3 0.2 -1.55}}
    {cameraDistance 1.6}
    {cameraPitch 26.0}
    {cameraYaw 22.0}}}

${cue "key" {' {callout "ssao-radius"}}}
The spiral's radius is five percent of the half-resolution image's height.

At ten eighty, that is twenty-seven half-resolution pixels.

${cue "key" {' {glide cameraDistance 4.5 sentence}}}
${cue "key" {' {callout "ssao-radius"}}}
So the radius does not scale with distance.

As the camera pulls back, the same disc on screen reaches much farther across the room.

${overCallout
    {' {ssaoRadiusRange 0.02 0.1}}
    "ssao-defaults"
    "A larger radius spreads the darkening wider and softer."}

### One minus AO, to the power 2.2, blurred and upsampled {#c7-output kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoSsao panelValues 0.4 {'
    {labels 0.0}
    viewModeFinal
    {ssao 1.0}
    {ssaoSamples 0.0}
    {ssaoRadius 0.05}}}}
${cue "key" {'
    {cameraTarget {' -0.3 0.3 -1.6}}
    {cameraDistance 2.6}
    {cameraPitch 22.0}
    {cameraYaw 18.0}}}

${cue "key" {'
    {inset "raw"}
    imageFromSsaoStage
    {imageStage 1.0}
    imageCornerTopLeft
    {imageTitle "views.ssao_stage"}}}
${overCallout
    {' {ssaoStrengthRange 0.5 1.5}}
    "ssao-compute"
    "The pass multiplies the occlusion by a strength setting, and stores one minus the result."}

That value is raised to the power two point two, and kept in eight bits.

${cue "key" {' {ssaoStrength 1.0} {blend 1.0}}}
${cue "key" {'
    {inset "blur"}
    imageFromSsaoStage
    {imageStage 2.0}
    imageCornerTopLeft
    {imageTitle "views.ssao_stage"}}}
${overCallout
    {' {ssaoBlurRange 0.0 1.0}}
    "blur-step"
    "A depth-aware blur smooths the noise that the fixed sample pattern leaves."}

It reaches eight pixels each way, and it stops at depth edges.

${cue "key" {'
    {inset "up"}
    imageFromSsaoStage
    {imageStage 3.0}
    imageCornerTopLeft
    {imageTitle "views.ssao_stage"}}}
${cue "key" {' {callout "depth-upsample"}}}
An upsample then brings the result back to full resolution.

It weighs the four nearest values by how close their depth is, so the dark does not bleed across a silhouette.

$settle ${cue "key" {'
    {inset "raw"}
    {mark 0.0 0.0 1.0 1.0}
    {imageMarkText "views.mark_raw"}
    {blend 0.6}}} The first view is the raw half-resolution result, with the fixed pattern's noise.

$settle ${cue "key" {'
    {inset "blur"}
    {mark 0.0 0.0 1.0 1.0}
    {imageMarkText "views.mark_blurred"}
    {blend 0.6}}} The next view is after the depth-aware blur, smoother but with its edges still sharp.

$settle ${cue "key" {'
    {inset "up"}
    {mark 0.0 0.0 1.0 1.0}
    {imageMarkText "views.mark_upsampled"}
    {blend 0.6}}} The last view is the full-resolution upsample, which the diffuse light is multiplied by.

### Finding: SSAO multiplies all diffuse light, direct too {#c7-where kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoRoom panelHidden 0.4 {append baseLight {'
    {labels 0.0}
    {forwardStage 2.0}}}}}
${cue "key" {'
    {cameraTarget {' -0.3 0.3 -1.6}}
    {cameraDistance 2.6}
    {cameraPitch 22.0}
    {cameraYaw 18.0}}}

${overCallout
    {' {forwardStageRange 2.0 3.0}}
    "ssao-modulate"
    "The occlusion multiplies the whole diffuse target, which holds the sun's direct light as well as the sky's."}

${cue "key" {' {glide sunYaw 80.0 sentence}}}
${cue "key" {' {callout "ssao-modulate"}}}
So even the sunlit side of a crease darkens.

The specular target, and the emission in it, are left alone.

${cue "key" {' {callout "ssao-modulate"}}}
The way the passes are connected shows that this is not ambient-only occlusion.

O3DE applies it to all diffuse light.

### A material's occlusion, an exponent on a map {#c7-material kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoSurface panelValues 0.5 {'
    {labels 1.0}
    {sphereOcclusion 1.0}
    {diffuseOcclusionFactor 1.0}}}}
${cue "key" {' {motion {' {cameraPath surfaceSweep}}}}}

${overCallout
    {' {specularOcclusionFactorRange 0.0 2.0}}
    "occlusion-input"
    "A material can also carry the occlusion maps of chapter three, one for diffuse light and one for specular."}

Each map is raised to the power of its factor.

${overCallout
    {' {diffuseOcclusionFactorRange 0.0 2.0}}
    "occlusion-finalize"
    "They multiply the diffuse and specular light at the end of the forward pass, direct light included."}

The albedo target also carries these factors to the full-screen passes that add the sky's light.

${cue "key" {' {callout "forward-ibl-ao-twice"}}}
For transparent materials and Skin, the sky's light is added inside the forward pass.

Inside the forward pass, the diffuse occlusion is applied to that light twice, once on its way in and once at the end.

### Occlusion, from two sources {#c7-recap kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoSsao panelHidden 1.0 {' {labels 0.0} viewModeFinal {ssao 1.0}}}}
${cue "key" {'
    {motion {' {cameraPath {' {pose 18.0 22.0 3.0} {pose 4.0 18.0 3.4} {pose 16.0 24.0 2.8}}}}}}}

${cue "key" {'
    {inset "up"}
    imageFromSsaoStage
    {imageStage 3.0}
    imageCornerTopLeft
    {imageTitle "views.ssao_stage"}}}
So occlusion comes from the screen, every frame, and from the material's maps, both as multipliers.

The screen's occlusion is cheap and needs no data.

But it darkens direct light too, and it ignores what lies off screen.

$settle So far, light has only bounced off surfaces.

$settle The next part lets it pass through them.

