## Light through surfaces {#c8 number=8 target=355 summary="Transmission in the Enhanced and Skin lighting models (thick objects after Barré-Brisebois and Bouchard, thin ones after Jimenez), the shadow-map thickness it reads and its units, what a leaf is in O3DE, screen-space subsurface scattering, and the Skin and Eye models." sources="skin-lighting-defines, sss-sample-count, transmission-option, back-lighting, transmission-params, transmission-kernel, shadow-thickness, shadow-thickness-camera-span, transmission-finalize, foliage-sample-material, double-sided-cull, back-face-normal, vertex-normal-unflipped, sss-pack, sss-kernel, skin-diffuse, eye-direct-lighting"}

### Only Enhanced and Skin let light through {#c8-which kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoTransmission panelValues 0.5 {'
    {labels 0.0}
    {sunLux 40.0}
    {iblExposure 1.0}
    {skyboxExposure 2.0}
    {exposure 0.35}
    {sunYaw 10.0}
    {sunPitch 22.0}
    {sphereLight 0.0}
    {walls 0.0}
    {teapotTransmission 0.0}}}}
${cue "key" {'
    {motion {' {cameraPath {' {pose 35.0 6.0 1.8} {pose 48.0 4.0 2.0} {pose 40.0 8.0 1.7}}}}}}}

Light that passes through a surface is called transmission.

${cue "key" {' {motion {' {path {' sunYaw} {' {' 30.0} {' 10.0}} sentence}}}}}
${cue "key" {' {callout "transmission-option"}}}
In O3DE, transmission is compiled only into the Enhanced and Skin lighting models.

StandardPBR, the default, has none.

${overCallout
    {' {teapotTransmissionRange 0.0 1.0}}
    "transmission-params"
    "In an EnhancedPBR material, transmission is off by default, in the mode None."}

The two other modes are thick object and thin object.

### Thick objects: light from behind, scattered toward the eye {#c8-thick kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoTransmission panelValues 0.4 {'
    {labels 0.0}
    {sunLux 40.0}
    {iblExposure 1.0}
    {skyboxExposure 2.0}
    {exposure 0.35}
    {sunYaw 10.0}
    {sunPitch 22.0}
    {sphereLight 0.0}
    {walls 0.0}
    {teapotTransmission 1.0}}}}
${cue "key" {' {motion {' {cameraPath transmissionSweep}}}}}

${cue "key" {' {motion {' {path {' sunYaw} {' {' -20.0} {' 30.0} {' 10.0}} sentence}}}}}
${cue "key" {' panelCode listingTransmission {highlightCode "transmission" {' "thick"}}}}
${cue "key" {' {callout "back-lighting"}}}
The thick object mode, after Barré-Brisebois and Bouchard, brightens where the eye looks toward the light through the object.

${overCallout
    {' {transmissionPowerRange 2.0 16.0}}
    "back-lighting"
    "The model measures how directly the eye looks toward the light, with the light's direction bent a little by the normal."}

That alignment is raised to a power.

A higher power gathers the glow more tightly toward the light's direction.

${cue "key" {' {transmissionPower 6.0} {blend 1.0}}}
${overCallout
    {' {transmissionThicknessRange 0.05 0.9}}
    "back-lighting"
    "The glow fades as the object gets thicker, by the exponential of minus four times the thickness."}

The glow is also multiplied by one minus the thickness, so it reaches zero at a thickness of one.

### Thin objects: the back face's light, through a kernel {#c8-thin kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoTransmission panelValues 0.4 {'
    {labels 0.0}
    {sunLux 40.0}
    {iblExposure 1.0}
    {skyboxExposure 2.0}
    {exposure 0.35}
    {sunYaw 10.0}
    {sunPitch 22.0}
    {sphereLight 0.0}
    {walls 0.0}
    {teapotTransmission 2.0}
    {transmissionThickness 0.5}}}}
${cue "key" {' {motion {' {cameraPath transmissionSweep}}}}}

${cue "key" {' {motion {' {path {' sunPitch} {' {' 10.0} {' 22.0}} sentence}}}}}
${cue "key" {' panelCode listingTransmission {highlightCode "transmission" {' "thin"}}}}
${cue "key" {' {callout "back-lighting"}}}
The thin model, after Jimenez, starts from the light falling on the object's far side, its back face.

That light is the albedo times the back face's N dot L.

${overCallout
    {' {transmissionThicknessRange 0.1 1.0}}
    "transmission-kernel"
    "The thin model then weakens that light by a falloff that depends on the measured thickness and on the scatter distance."}

The scatter distance is how far light travels inside the material, and it is set per colour channel.

${cue "key" {' {transmissionThickness 0.5} {blend 1.0}}}
${cue "key" {' {callout "transmission-params"}}}
The default scatter distance is eight for red, two point one six for green, and one point zero four for blue.

So red passes much farther than blue, and the glow turns orange.

${overCallout
    {' {sunYawRange 10.0 60.0}}
    "back-lighting"
    "As the sun moves from behind the teapot to its side, the back face turns away from it and the glow fades."}

### Thickness from the shadow map, and its units {#c8-thickness kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoTransmission panelValues 0.3 {'
    {labels 0.0}
    {sunLux 40.0}
    {iblExposure 1.0}
    {skyboxExposure 2.0}
    {exposure 0.35}
    {sunYaw 10.0}
    {sunPitch 22.0}
    {sphereLight 0.0}
    {walls 0.0}
    {teapotTransmission 2.0}}}}
${cue "key" {' {motion {' {cameraPath transmissionSweep}}}}}

${cue "key" {'
    {inset "cm"}
    imageFromCascadeMap
    {imageCascade 4.0}
    {imageParamFrom 0.9}
    imageCornerBottomLeft
    {imageTitle "views.cascade_map"}}}
${cue "key" {' {callout "shadow-thickness"}}}
The thickness comes from the sun's cascade.

It is the depth between the shaded point and the first surface the sun meets.

In the cascade view, the ring marks the teapot's point.

The depth stored there belongs to the side facing the sun, and the point's own depth is the far side.

${cue "key" {' {callout "shadow-thickness-camera-span"}}}
The code shows that this difference is measured in the cascade's own depth range.

But the code multiplies it by the camera's depth span instead, so the result is not in metres.

${cue "key" {' {callout "shadow-thickness"}}}
A flat, thin surface like a leaf gets two shifts when its thickness is measured.

With the default biases, the normal offset of chapter five and an inward shift of the thin mode nearly cancel.

So the measured thickness is about zero, and the glow runs at full strength.

${overCallout
    {' {transmissionScaleRange 0.0 1.0}}
    "transmission-finalize"
    "The transmitted light is tinted and added to the diffuse light after the material's occlusion, so that occlusion never darkens it."}

### A leaf in O3DE is a material, not a lighting model {#c8-foliage kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoTransmission panelValues 0.5 {'
    {labels 0.0}
    {sunLux 40.0}
    {iblExposure 1.0}
    {skyboxExposure 2.0}
    {exposure 0.35}
    {sunYaw 10.0}
    {sunPitch 22.0}
    {sphereLight 0.0}
    {walls 0.0}
    {teapotTransmission 0.0}}}}
${cue "key" {'
    {motion {' {cameraPath {' {pose 35.0 6.0 1.8} {pose 48.0 4.0 2.0} {pose 40.0 8.0 1.7}}}}}}}

${cue "key" {' {callout "foliage-sample-material"}}} O3DE has no foliage shading of its own.

Its vegetation system places ordinary meshes, with whatever material they carry.

${cue "key" {' {motion {' {path {' sunYaw} {' {' 40.0} {' 10.0}} sentence}}}}}
${cue "key" {' {callout "cutout-clip"}}}
A plain leaf is StandardPBR, cut out by its alpha and drawn double-sided.

Lit from behind, it stays dark, because Standard has no transmission.

${overCallout
    {' {teapotTransmissionRange 0.0 2.0}}
    "foliage-sample-material"
    "O3DE's own leaf-like test material is EnhancedPBR, double-sided, in thin mode with a green tint."}

The lab shows that model on its teapot.

${cue "key" {' {callout "vertex-normal-unflipped"}}}
On a back face, the shading normal, the one the lighting uses, is flipped, but the mesh's vertex normal is not.

Transmission and the shadow offset both use that unflipped vertex normal.

### Subsurface scattering: a blur of the diffuse light {#c8-sss kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoTransmission panelHidden 0.5 {'
    {labels 0.0}
    {sunLux 40.0}
    {iblExposure 1.0}
    {skyboxExposure 2.0}
    {exposure 0.35}
    {sunYaw 60.0}
    {sunPitch 40.0}
    {sphereLight 0.0}
    {walls 1.0}
    {teapotTransmission 0.0}}}}
${cue "key" {'
    {motion {' {cameraPath {' {pose 35.0 6.0 1.8} {pose 32.0 5.0 1.8} {pose 38.0 8.0 1.7}}}}}}}

${cue "key" {' {callout "enhanced-draw-list"}}}
Subsurface scattering spreads light under the surface, so it leaves a little away from where it entered.

Enhanced and Skin materials with scattering on write one more target, the scatter distance.

They also pack a scattering factor, for how strong the scattering is, and a quality setting into the diffuse target's alpha.

${cue "key" {' {callout "sss-pack"}}}
The pack is the factor's byte times two hundred and fifty-six, plus the quality's byte.

That integer is stored in a half float, which holds integers exactly only up to two thousand and forty-eight, so larger packs come back rounded.

${cue "key" {' {callout "sss-kernel"}}}
A compute pass then blurs the diffuse light with Burley's normalised diffusion profile, a curve of how far light spreads under the surface.

It takes two hundred samples a pixel at full quality, and eighty at the default quality of zero point four.

The blur's footprint is set by the scatter distance, in millimetres.

${cue "key" {' {callout "sss-sample-count"}}} The lab does not run this pass.

Its targets carry a packed alpha of minus one, which means no scattering, for every surface.

### Skin and Eye {#c8-skin kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoDiffuse panelValues 0.4 {' {vectors 0.0} {lobe 0.0} viewModeFinal}}}
${cue "key" {' {motion {' {cameraPath brdfSweep}}}}}

${over
    {' {sphereRoughnessRange 0.3 0.9}}
    {cat {'
        {text " "}
        {cue "key" {' diffuseModelNormalizedDisney}}
        {text " "}
        {cue "key" {' {callout "skin-diffuse"}}}
        {text " Skin always uses Disney's normalised diffuse, rough or not."}}}}

It also offers a second specular lobe, blended with the first.

${cue "key" {' {motion {' {path {' cameraPitch} {' {' 40.0} {' 6.0}} sentence}}}}}
${cue "key" {' {callout "skin-lighting-defines"}}}
Skin takes the sky's light inside the forward pass, and uses a human skin profile for thin transmission.

${cue "key" {' diffuseModelLambertian}}
${cue "key" {'
    {motion {' {eased easingSine {orbit {' lightX lightY} {' -1.05 1.24} 60.0 sentence}}}}}}
${cue "key" {' {callout "eye-direct-lighting"}}}
Eye uses the Skin model's material setup, but StandardPBR's direct lighting.

Its iris looks refracted, because the shader shifts the iris texture inside a sphere.

### Light that passes through {#c8-recap kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoTransmission panelHidden 1.0 {'
    {labels 0.0}
    {sunLux 40.0}
    {iblExposure 1.0}
    {skyboxExposure 2.0}
    {exposure 0.35}
    {sunYaw 10.0}
    {sunPitch 22.0}
    {sphereLight 0.0}
    {walls 0.0}
    {teapotTransmission 2.0}}}}
${cue "key" {'
    {motion {'
        {cameraPath {' {pose 35.0 6.0 1.8} {pose 48.0 4.0 2.0} {pose 40.0 8.0 1.7}}}
        {path {' sunYaw} {' {' 40.0} {' 10.0}} beat}}}}}

So transmission is extra light from behind, added per light inside the forward pass, and only for Enhanced and Skin materials.

Transmission reuses the sun's shadow map for thickness, which keeps it cheap and makes its scale approximate.

$settle Every surface so far went through a material type.

$settle Terrain brings its own forward shader.

### Where to read {#c8-sources kind=2d sources="transmission-option, transmission-params, back-lighting, transmission-kernel, shadow-thickness, shadow-thickness-camera-span, transmission-finalize, foliage-sample-material, cutout-clip, vertex-normal-unflipped, enhanced-draw-list, sss-pack, sss-kernel, skin-diffuse, eye-direct-lighting"}

These are the files and lines this chapter followed, in reading order.

## Terrain, and a word on hair {#c9 number=9 target=265 summary="The Terrain gem's own forward shader: fixed macro values, normals made per vertex on the CPU, detail materials blended by weight, multiscatter in the sky's light but not in direct light; and the Marschner model of the AtomTressFX hair gem." sources="opaque-pass-order, terrain-pass-request, terrain-forward-draw-list, terrain-macro-constants, terrain-normal-cpu, terrain-normal-ps, terrain-height-blend, terrain-height-blend-option, terrain-no-direct-multiscatter, terrain-multiscatter-flags, hair-default-model, hair-marschner"}

### Terrain draws through the forward pass with its own shader {#c9-own kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoTerrain panelValues 0.8 {'
    {labels 0.0}
    viewModeFinal
    {terrainO3deNormal 0.0}
    {forwardStage 3.0}}}}
${cue "key" {'
    {motion {'
        {cameraPath {' {pose 30.0 28.0 22.0} {pose 10.0 22.0 24.0} {pose 36.0 32.0 20.0}}}}}}}

Terrain is drawn with a forward shader of its own.

${cue "key" {chainTarget "ta" 2.0 imageCornerTopLeft {' {imageTitle "views.target_albedo"}}}}
${cue "key" {' {callout "terrain-forward-draw-list"}}}
The Terrain gem's material type lists its own forward, depth, and shadow shaders.

They draw into the same forward pass, and into the same five targets.

$settle ${cue "key" {'
    {inset "ta"}
    {mark 0.0 0.18 1.0 0.92}
    {imageMarkText "views.mark_hills"}
    {blend 0.6}}} In the albedo view, the hills sit in the same target as everything else, in their macro colour, the coarse colour that covers the whole terrain.

$settle So the later passes read a terrain pixel exactly as they read the room's.

${cue "key" {' {inset "ta"} unmark}}
${overCallout
    {' {forwardStageRange 0.0 3.0}}
    "opaque-pass-order"
    "The sky's light, the reflections, and the occlusion likewise reach the hills through the same full-screen passes as everything else."}

${cue "key" {' {imageHide {' "ta"}}}}
${cue "key" {' {callout "terrain-pass-request"}}}
The terrain's feature processor, the engine part that manages it, adds only compute passes.

Those passes fill clipmaps, caches of the terrain's textures that are finest near the camera, and they are off by default.

${cue "key" {' {glide sunYaw 170.0 sentence}}}
${cue "key" {' {callout "terrain-forward-draw-list"}}}
The terrain casts and receives the sun's shadows like any opaque surface, and reads the same shadow buffer.

### Fixed macro values: roughness one, F zero four percent {#c9-fixed kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoTerrain panelValues 0.5 {'
    {labels 0.0}
    viewModeFinal
    {paramFormula 0.0}
    {sunYaw 120.0}
    {sunPitch 35.0}}}}
${cue "key" {'
    {motion {'
        {cameraPath {' {pose 30.0 18.0 16.0} {pose 14.0 14.0 18.0} {pose 36.0 20.0 15.0}}}}}}}

${cue "key" {' {term 1}}}
${cue "key" {' {callout "terrain-macro-constants"}}}
The macro material is the coarse one that covers the whole terrain.

Where only the macro material covers the ground, the shader fixes roughness at one and metalness at zero.

${cue "key" {' {term 2}}}
${cue "key" {' viewModeSpecular}}
${overCallout
    {' {sunPitchRange 35.0 8.0}}
    "terrain-macro-constants"
    "Its specular factor of a half gives an F zero of zero point zero four."}

So even with the sun low, the hills keep only a broad, faint sheen.

${cue "key" {' {term 3}}}
${cue "key" {' viewModeFinal {sunPitch 35.0} {blend 1.5}}}
${cue "key" {' {callout "terrain-no-direct-multiscatter"}}}
The terrain's lighting data never computes the multiscatter compensation of chapter four.

So its direct light uses a compensation factor of one.

Even so, the flag in its normal target, the one from chapter one, still asks the sky's reflection pass for compensation.

${overCallout
    {' {multiscatterRange 1.0 0.0}}
    "terrain-multiscatter-flags"
    "Turning the flag off changes only the sky's reflection on the hills."}

Their direct light was never compensated.

${cue "key" {' {multiscatter 1.0} {blend 1.0}}}
${cue "key" {' {callout "terrain-height-blend-option"}}}
Up close, detail materials replace the macro one, blended by plain weights.

They could also blend by height instead, but that option is off by default.

The lab's hills have no detail materials.

### Normals made on the CPU, per vertex, in eight bits {#c9-normal kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoTerrain panelValues 0.4 {'
    {labels 0.0}
    viewModeFinal
    {terrainO3deNormal 0.0}
    {sunYaw 120.0}
    {sunPitch 20.0}}}}
${cue "key" {'
    {motion {'
        {cameraPath {' {pose 30.0 34.0 18.0} {pose 14.0 30.0 20.0} {pose 36.0 36.0 17.0}}}}}}}

${cue "key" {' {callout "terrain-normal-cpu"}}}
The terrain's normals are computed on the CPU, at each vertex, from central differences of the heights.

${cue "key" {' {glide sunYaw 60.0 sentence}}}
${cue "key" {' {callout "terrain-normal-cpu"}}}
Each axis's slope is normalised on its own, by dividing it by the square root of one plus the slope squared.

The result is stored as an eight bit signed number.

${overCallout
    {' {terrainO3deNormalRange 0.0 1.0}}
    "terrain-normal-ps"
    "The vertex shader rebuilds the up component from the two stored slopes, as the square root of one minus their squares."}

That is exact where the ground slopes along one axis, and wrong where it slopes both ways.

${cue "key" {' viewModeTerrainNormalError}}
${cue "key" {'
    {motion {' {path {' cameraYaw cameraPitch} {' {' 60.0 40.0} {' 10.0 30.0}} sentence}}}}}
${cue "key" {' {callout "terrain-normal-ps"}}}
This view shows the angle between O3DE's normal and the exact one on the lab's hills.

The angle is near zero on gentle ground, and many degrees on the steepest two-way slopes.

${cue "key" {' viewModeFinal}}
${cue "key" {' {glide sunYaw 150.0 sentence}}}
Without a macro normal map, a coarse normal texture for the whole terrain, this interpolated vertex normal is the only normal the terrain has.

The sun's shading follows that normal.

### Hair: three Marschner lobes {#c9-hair kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoRoom panelHidden 1.0 {append baseLight {'
    {labels 0.0}
    {forwardStage 3.0}}}}}
${cue "key" {'
    {motion {' {cameraPath {' {pose 20.0 20.0 6.2} {pose 0.0 14.0 6.6} {pose 16.0 18.0 6.0}}}}}}}

${cue "key" {' {callout "hair-pass-after-opaque"}}}
Hair, from the AtomTressFX gem, is drawn by passes of its own after the opaque frame, and composited over it.

${cue "key" {' {callout "hair-default-model"}}}
Its default lighting is Marschner's model, which sums three lobes.

R reflects off the strand's surface, TT passes through the strand, and TRT reflects off its inside.

The light is their sum, ${formulaSaid "R + TT + TRT" "R plus T T plus T R T" {'}}.

${cue "key" {' {callout "hair-marschner"}}}
Each lobe is a Gaussian along the strand, times a second term for the angle around the strand.

The model uses a refractive index of one point five five, and tilts the cuticle, the strand's scaly surface, by zero point zero eight radians.

The lab has no strands to show it on.

### Special geometry, the same frame {#c9-recap kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoTerrain panelHidden 1.0 {'
    {labels 0.0}
    viewModeFinal
    {terrainO3deNormal 1.0}}}}
${cue "key" {'
    {motion {'
        {cameraPath {' {pose 30.0 28.0 22.0} {pose 6.0 22.0 24.0} {pose 36.0 32.0 20.0}}}
        {path {' sunYaw} {' {' 160.0} {' 100.0}} beat}}}}}

So terrain brings its own shader, but it writes the same targets and reads the same lights and shadows.

Hair lights itself in passes of its own, after the opaque frame.

All that is left is to follow one pixel back through every step it took.

